WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:08.010
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:11.220
Thank you so much for
joining us both in person

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.710
and on Zoom hybrid.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.714
Let me start by thanking
Professor Carole McGranahan

00:00:16.714 --> 00:00:20.269
for joining us at the
refugee seminar series

00:00:20.269 --> 00:00:25.269
and thanking the capable
help of Teresa Delgado

00:00:26.335 --> 00:00:28.830
for arranging this visit.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:33.830
It is an like a really wonderful
honor to have Carole join.

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:36.720
She's been a mentor,

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.715
and she is one of the
stars of anthropology

00:00:39.715 --> 00:00:42.240
and the refugee studies.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.270
Professor McGranahan is
a cultural anthropologist

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:49.017
and historian specializing
in contemporary Tibet.

00:00:49.017 --> 00:00:52.890
Her research focuses on issues
on colonialism and empire,

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.950
history and memory, power and politics,

00:00:55.950 --> 00:01:00.540
refugees and citizenship,
nationalism, senses of belonging,

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:05.100
gender, war, and anthropology
as theoretical storytelling.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.123
Since '94, she has conducted research

00:01:07.123 --> 00:01:11.130
in Tibetan refugee
communities in India and Nepal

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:14.364
on the history and politics
of guerilla resistance

00:01:14.364 --> 00:01:17.370
between 1950 and 1974,

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.190
which culminated in her
book, "Arrested Histories:

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:25.190
Tibet, the CIA, and Memories
of a Forgotten War."

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.040
It came out in 2010 with
Duke University Press.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.880
Thinking of 20th and 21st
century Tibetan histories

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.551
and experiences as
imperial in a global sense

00:01:36.551 --> 00:01:40.380
is a key part of her work
in relation to the CIA,

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.740
British India and the
People's Republic of China.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:47.100
Professor McGranahan work
on Empire beyond Europe

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.670
can be found in an edited
volume, "Imperial Formations,"

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:52.293
which came out in 2007.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.290
It was co-edited with by Anne Staler

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:57.108
and Peter Perdue as well,

00:01:57.108 --> 00:01:59.177
and in "Ethnographies of U.S. Empire,"

00:01:59.177 --> 00:02:03.390
which came out in 2018,
co-edited with John Collins.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:06.300
Currently, Professor
McGranahan is completing a book

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:07.450
about the (indistinct).

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:09.459
- [Professor Carole] (indistinct).

00:02:09.459 --> 00:02:10.959
Thank you, family,

00:02:12.733 --> 00:02:14.540
Tibet and British India

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:17.340
in the first half of the 20th century

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.959
and she's finishing a decade
of research in France,

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:22.980
India, Nepal, New York City, Switzerland,

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.633
and Toronto titled, "Refugee Citizenship:

00:02:25.633 --> 00:02:28.454
Asylum Refusal and Political Subjectivity

00:02:28.454 --> 00:02:30.870
in the Tibetan Diaspora."

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:33.330
She's presenting from that research today,

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:36.750
a talk titled Refusal
as Political Practice:

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:40.050
an Ethnography of Citizenship
and Refugee Status.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:41.738
Please help me welcome her today.

00:02:41.738 --> 00:02:45.027
(crowd applauding)

00:02:45.027 --> 00:02:46.530
- I'd like to say thank you to Eda Pepi

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.350
and to everyone at Yale
for inviting me to be here.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:52.650
It's my pleasure to be able to
share this research with you.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:54.690
So the first time I went to Toronto,

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:57.420
all everyone was talking
about was political asylum

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.120
and gaining convention refugee status.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.950
But people weren't just talking about it,

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:03.570
they were joking about it.

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:06.600
There was one joke we
heard everywhere we went.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.467
It went something like this.

00:03:08.467 --> 00:03:10.320
"Have you heard about the Sherpa guy

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.260
who posed as a Tibetan in asylum court?

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.715
The judge asked him when the
Dalai Lama's birthday was,

00:03:15.715 --> 00:03:18.487
and the man answered, 'Saturday?'"

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.920
Everyone who told the
joke roared with laughter

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:23.973
at the punchline.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:26.273
Yup.

00:03:26.273 --> 00:03:28.950
No matter how many times it
was told to Kesang and me

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:31.350
in this friend's apartment or that one,

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:34.800
Kesang would laugh so hard
she had to wipe away tears.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.439
The joke was funny because,
of course, every Tibetan knows

00:03:38.439 --> 00:03:41.880
that the Dalai Lama's
birthday was July 6th.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.500
Yet in India and Nepal,

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:47.400
his birthday is usually not
celebrated exactly on the 6th,

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.770
but instead is celebrated on Saturday,

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.200
which is kind the national holiday.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.780
Kind of the one day holiday
in a six-day work week.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:56.550
The bida, the Chuti.

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.020
While, all Tibetans know this,

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:00.930
they also know that most
Sherpa's wouldn't know this

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:03.630
as the Sherpa community
typically does not celebrate

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:05.013
the Dalai Lama's birthday.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:07.170
For Kesang and her friends,

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:08.730
the joke was humorous commentary

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:12.030
on Tibetan and Himalayan
immigration to North America

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:14.880
on a shared experience
of the asylum system

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.500
and the political possibilities

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.023
embedded in the idea of citizenship.

00:04:19.986 --> 00:04:21.420
As an anthropologist on the alert

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:23.100
for a new research project,

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.585
when I heard the joke for the
fifth, sixth, seventh time,

00:04:25.585 --> 00:04:27.630
I knew this was it.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:28.740
In order to understand

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.870
why this was happening in Canada, however,

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.225
we also have to consider
the broader context,

00:04:33.225 --> 00:04:36.510
which is Tibetan's six decades
of refusing citizenship

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:37.890
in South Asia,

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:40.500
the history behind their
immigration to United States,

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.810
and then often onto Canada,

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:45.592
and why the Sherpa man
was posing as a Tibetan

00:04:45.592 --> 00:04:47.790
in the first place.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:50.130
25 years ago, if you drove or walked

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:52.500
past the US Embassy in Kathmandu,

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:54.840
you would've seen a long
queue of people lined up

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:56.640
outside it on some days.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:58.380
Many of them were Tibetan.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.030
And as gossip went,

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.830
some of those in line were
pretending they were Tibetan.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.140
They were young Tekkali
or Manangi or Sherpa men,

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.790
some of whom had shaved their heads,

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:12.480
dawned maroon robes and
pretended to be monks

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.300
based on a perceived notion
of Tibetan desirability

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:18.480
that they hoped would
get them a rare US visa.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:20.087
One day in 1998,

00:05:20.087 --> 00:05:23.010
Kesang and I stood in
the queue with a friend.

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:26.730
We calmed her nerves by talking
about the embassy official

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:29.370
everyone referred to as Mr. David.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:32.580
Supposedly, he was a Buddhist
and he liked Tibetans.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:34.650
And so if you were a Tibetan

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:38.280
or successfully convinced
embassy staff that you were one,

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.730
your chances of receiving a
visa were believed to be good.

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:44.400
Sought after in this
process was a tourist visa,

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.470
which one would enter the United States

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:48.960
and stay beyond its expiration

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:51.450
with the intention of somehow, some way

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:53.940
securing a job in the USA

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:56.880
so that you could send
money home to your family.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:58.880
After several hours of
waiting outside the embassy,

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:01.260
our friend emerged victorious.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.510
She had received a visa.

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:08.510
Although none of us knew it
back in that day on 1998,

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:10.140
almost 20 years later,

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:13.230
both she and Kesang would
be living in Toronto

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:15.060
as Canadian citizens.

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:16.680
In this span of time,

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:20.400
a major ship shift happened in
the Tibetan refugee community

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.170
with thousands of individuals

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:25.293
migrating from South
Asia to North America.

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:28.590
That first visit that I made to Toronto

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:30.510
wasn't only about jokes,

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:32.733
there was also serious reflection.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:38.520
All I have are stories, a book of stories.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:39.720
Pepe said this to me

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.750
as we sat in the Shangri-La
Tibetan Restaurant in Toronto

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:46.320
on a snowy December night in 2007.

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:48.713
I had asked about her
generation's experiences

00:06:48.713 --> 00:06:52.140
of immigrating from South
Asia to North America.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.330
Although she was now a 20-year-old woman,

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:55.440
we had known each other

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:58.020
since she was a six-year-old
girl in Kathmandu.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:59.880
And I was a young graduate student

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:01.200
just beginning my research

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:03.480
with the Tibetan refugee community.

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:05.790
She and her mother had
just moved to Canada,

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:08.190
and I was visiting them
there for the first time.

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:09.963
Again, this is in 2007.

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:14.280
Our belly is full of shabdak,
tingmo, and aloo phingsha.

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:16.950
We left the restaurant and
walked back to her apartment

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:19.830
where her mother, Kesang,
was waiting for us.

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:22.180
We greeted her and settled
in around the table.

00:07:23.705 --> 00:07:27.180
Pepe turned back to our
earlier conversation.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:30.360
The issues we are facing
are papers, money,

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:31.800
and rights, she said.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:34.710
Citizenship papers, money for everything,

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:36.510
and the right to protest.

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:37.897
Kesang joined and saying,

00:07:37.897 --> 00:07:40.110
"We didn't have any of those in Nepal."

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:42.960
Tibetans didn't have papers,
money, or rights there.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.780
She, referring to Pepe, was born in Nepal

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:47.850
but couldn't be a citizen.

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:49.260
Pepe was not alone in this.

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:52.230
For six decades, Tibetan
refugees have lived in South Asia

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:53.970
without citizenship.

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:56.656
Tibetans refused citizenship in South Asia

00:07:56.656 --> 00:07:59.760
to both claim sovereignty in Tibet

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:03.330
and to assert political
equality and interdependence,

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:06.360
vis-a-vis the governments
of India and Nepal.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:09.870
As Tibetans like Pepe and
Kesang moved to North America,

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:13.530
their refusal as political
practice changes in some ways,

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:16.230
though remains the same in others.

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:19.050
For people who have been
collectively dispossessed,

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:21.510
what does it mean to refuse

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:24.030
international norms of citizenship

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:27.333
by insisting on a sovereignty
that has been lost?

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:31.350
In the case of Tibet,

00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:33.630
refusing citizenship in South Asia

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:36.262
is a means of claiming past, present,

00:08:36.262 --> 00:08:38.940
and future state sovereignty.

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:43.140
Tibet was, Tibet is, Tibet will be.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:46.080
This claim challenges
globally hegemonic formulas

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:48.330
for sovereignty and citizenship.

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:50.640
It rejects the idea
that the state of Tibet

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.130
is merely a former political entity,

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:57.060
and instead asserts contemporary
political legitimacy.

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:00.510
Yet what happens to political
claims embedded in refusal

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:03.630
when refugees become citizens elsewhere?

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:05.970
How might refusal be reconfigured

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:10.020
to maintain political claims
to home and state sovereignty?

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:12.210
The seeming contradiction
of Tibetan refugees

00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:14.790
accepting citizenship in North America

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:17.760
after decades of refusing it in South Asia

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:21.540
demonstrates that hegemonic
categories and practices

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:24.960
need not be debilitating or total.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:26.520
Tibetans collective refusal

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:29.490
of an externally assigned political status

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:32.460
posits a different status in its place.

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:35.130
In so doing, it refuses the probable

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:36.933
in favor of the possible.

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:39.450
So in this paper,

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:41.520
I argue that the unfolding history

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:43.440
of the Tibetan refugee citizenship,

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:47.280
documentation, and migration
challenges contemporary norms

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:49.860
and possibilities for political status.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:51.910
Tibetans in exile do so first

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:56.670
by inhabiting to ostensibly
oppose categories

00:09:56.670 --> 00:09:58.380
that of refugee and citizen,

00:09:58.380 --> 00:09:59.940
and they do so second

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:03.690
by refusing to accept their
sovereignty as past tense.

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:05.100
That is.

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:06.420
Their current status,

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:09.390
regardless of whether it
is refugee or undocumented

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:12.390
or stateless or citizen,
rests in a temporality

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:15.390
guided as much by a Buddhist
sense of impermanence

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:18.810
as by an irreverence
for international norms.

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:20.580
Strategies might change,

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:22.650
but the goal remains the same,

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:25.503
regaining Tibetan sovereignty in Tibet.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:31.020
So I also want to argue that
ethnographic knowledge is vital

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:34.080
for understanding experiences of refugees.

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:36.876
That is alongside political, historic,

00:10:36.876 --> 00:10:39.240
or legal knowledge, for example.

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:41.880
Ethnography provides us with a unique

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:44.340
and complimentary way of knowing.

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:46.350
Specifically, ethnographic research

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:49.410
gives us conceptual
insights to life as lived,

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:51.030
to how structures feel,

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:53.220
to how categories are experienced,

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:57.120
and importantly, into worlds
of both action and agency,

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:00.390
which for so long have
been denied to refugees.

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:02.250
My research with the Tibetan community

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:03.960
spans over three decades

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:06.640
and involves personal and
political commitments,

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:09.720
as well as the relationships
and ethnographies that are...

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:12.240
Sorry, relationships and obligations

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:14.760
that are at the heart of ethnography.

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:17.790
Kesang and I first met in 1994.

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:18.750
You can see us here.

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:21.780
This is outside of the
airport in Kathmandu.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:24.120
She and I have been close ever since,

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:26.730
sharing joys and sorrows
over three decades

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:28.560
in three separate countries,

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:30.510
including just a few weeks ago.

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:32.430
You can see the photo
here about a decade ago.

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:34.920
We're at Niagara Falls.

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:39.060
And then this photo which was
taken in October of this year.

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:41.340
My second argument is about refusal

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:43.500
as 21st century intervention

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:46.980
into possibilities for
citizenship and sovereignty.

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:49.830
By this, I do not mean the
refusal of immigration judges

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:51.960
or officials who denied passage,

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:55.470
denied recognition,
asylum, status to people.

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:58.080
Instead, I mean refusal as a willful,

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:00.360
conscious political act.

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:03.150
To refuse can be generative and strategic,

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:05.790
a deliberate move toward one thing,

00:12:05.790 --> 00:12:08.130
belief, practice, or community,

00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:09.663
and away from another.

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:15.090
The generative aspect of
refusal recognizes the sociality

00:12:15.090 --> 00:12:19.110
of saying no in order to
build something different.

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:21.600
This affiliative aspect
is one of the things

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:24.120
that links refusal to resistance,

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:27.660
but refusal is not a
synonym for resistance,

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:29.968
where resistance involves
consciously defying

00:12:29.968 --> 00:12:32.850
or opposing superiors

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:35.940
in a context of differential
power relationships

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:37.980
between state and society,

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:41.250
refusal rejects such a hierarchy,

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:42.780
also rejects subjugation

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:45.300
as the position from
which action is taken.

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:50.100
Instead, refusal reposits
relationships as between equals,

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:53.313
insisting inequity rather than inequity.

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:56.790
And so here in referring to refusal,

00:12:56.790 --> 00:12:58.170
I draw on the important research

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:00.090
of anthropologists, Audra Simpson,

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:03.330
for whom refusal is both
ethnographic and political,

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:05.550
both stance and theory.

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:07.890
Simpson is one of the
scholars in indigenous studies

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:09.780
leading the way and theorizing refusal.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:10.970
I think you're also,

00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:12.990
of the work of scholars such as Eve Tuck

00:13:12.990 --> 00:13:16.590
and Wayne Yang, Kim Talbert.

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:17.670
However, around the same time,

00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:20.520
we also see refusal being
theorized in other fields.

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:21.690
We see it in black studies,

00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:23.520
and especially in black feminist studies

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:25.590
with scholars such as Tina Campt,

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:28.470
Gina Athena Ulysse, Savannah Shange

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:32.280
We see it in pre pandemic
research on vaccine refusal.

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:34.290
And, of course, we see
it in current research

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:35.700
about the pandemic,

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:38.100
as well as in contemporary
populous politics

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:40.860
such as the January 6th insurrection.

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:43.410
We see it in work with
conscientious objectors.

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:45.420
We see it in research with refugees

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:48.840
who reject the norms of the
international refugee regime.

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:51.630
And this last one is the Tibetan stance,

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:54.390
a position enacted in a very specific

00:13:54.390 --> 00:13:56.913
and unusual political context.

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:01.830
In order to understand what is happening

00:14:01.830 --> 00:14:04.140
with Tibetan immigration in North America,

00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:05.970
it's important to understand the history

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:09.120
of the Tibetan refugee
community in South Asia.

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:12.540
So in 1949, Mao Zedong's
People Liberation Army

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:14.190
marched into Tibet.

00:14:14.190 --> 00:14:17.250
By 1951, had taken over the country.

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:18.630
In the mid-1950s,

00:14:18.630 --> 00:14:21.210
Tibetans began to fight
back in eastern Tibet.

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:25.950
By 1959, the fighting reached
Lhasa, Tibet's capital.

00:14:25.950 --> 00:14:28.620
The Dalai Lama, Tibet's
political and religious leader

00:14:28.620 --> 00:14:31.740
escaped into exile in India

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:34.500
and thousands of Tibetans followed him.

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:36.360
Once in India, the Dalai Lama's government

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:38.850
immediately reconstituted itself.

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:43.290
It retains today the
uncontested leadership

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:45.300
of the Tibetan community.

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:48.180
The Tibetan government in exile
is a functioning government.

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:51.240
It has an elected parliament,
it has a court system,

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:54.120
it conducts a census, it collects taxes,

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:55.800
it runs its own school,

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:58.830
it maintains hegemonic
practices of belonging,

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:01.010
and it is the sole representative

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:05.910
of over 140,000 Tibetans
resident in India and Nepal,

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:08.760
as well as elsewhere around the world.

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:10.680
Although citizenship has been debated

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:12.151
at different times in exile history,

00:15:12.151 --> 00:15:15.600
it was in the early 1960s,
again, in the 1970s,

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:17.820
and most recently in the last decade

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:20.400
that exile government has
long refused citizenship

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:23.913
in South Asia as part of its
political claim to Tibet.

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:28.170
However, this refusal is complicated.

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:31.590
No country in South Asia,
not India, not Nepal,

00:15:31.590 --> 00:15:33.960
not any other South Asian state

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:37.620
are signatories to the 1951 UN Convention

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:39.720
relating to the status of refugees

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:44.469
or to the 1967 Protocol
which updated the convention.

00:15:44.469 --> 00:15:46.730
What is thus typical of many countries

00:15:46.730 --> 00:15:50.040
is not the norm in South Asia.

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:51.600
Applying for and receiving

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:54.990
UN designated convention refugee status

00:15:54.990 --> 00:15:57.750
sets in motion a series
of rights and protections

00:15:57.750 --> 00:15:59.460
guaranteed by the state,

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:01.860
including a path to citizenship

00:16:01.860 --> 00:16:03.810
rather than a return to a country

00:16:03.810 --> 00:16:06.003
where one would face political threats.

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:09.360
In South Asia, Tibetans are refugees

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:10.770
without the opportunity

00:16:10.770 --> 00:16:13.020
to apply for convention refugee status,

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:17.490
whether they are born in
Tibet, in India, or in Nepal.

00:16:17.490 --> 00:16:20.850
As a result, six decades of families,

00:16:20.850 --> 00:16:25.197
multiple generations live in
South Asia as undocumented

00:16:25.197 --> 00:16:28.170
or underdocumented peoples.

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:30.810
There is no path to citizenship for them,

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:33.513
and this is unusual in the world.

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:37.980
One tension of exile sovereignty

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:40.123
is that individuals lack identification

00:16:40.123 --> 00:16:42.630
and life event documents.

00:16:42.630 --> 00:16:45.690
Tibetans in South Asia
hold either fake documents

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:48.150
or locally issued old fashioned ones

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:51.450
not widely recognized
outside of South Asia,

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:54.180
or they have no documents at all.

00:16:54.180 --> 00:16:56.490
In India, most but not all Tibetans

00:16:56.490 --> 00:16:59.334
hold renewable Foreigner's
Registration Certificates,

00:16:59.334 --> 00:17:01.290
referred to as RCs,

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:05.460
and identification certificates
referred to as ICs.

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:07.440
In Nepal, the situation is worse

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:10.590
as the government has never
had an official program

00:17:10.590 --> 00:17:12.690
to identify and provide documentation

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:14.730
to all Tibetan refugees.

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:17.620
Although at various points up until 1989,

00:17:17.620 --> 00:17:21.123
it did issue some
documents to some Tibetans.

00:17:22.110 --> 00:17:23.610
The United Nations High Commission

00:17:23.610 --> 00:17:25.920
for Refugees Office in Kathmandu

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:28.350
assists as a transit facility

00:17:28.350 --> 00:17:30.480
for Tibetans who have escaped from Tibet

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:33.060
and are traveling on to India.

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:34.800
So unlike in Palestine,

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:37.110
the United Nations does
not offer any identity

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:40.020
or travel documentation to Tibetans

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:44.850
or have any Tibet-specific
offices in India or Nepal.

00:17:44.850 --> 00:17:48.030
The only document available
to all Tibetan refugees

00:17:48.030 --> 00:17:50.640
is the Green Book issued
by the exile government,

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:52.440
and that is what you can
see on the screen now.

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:53.730
These are the green books.

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:55.440
They are shaped like passports,

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:57.603
but they cannot be used like one.

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:00.270
The history of possibilities

00:18:00.270 --> 00:18:03.720
for Tibetan citizenship
in South Asia is unclear.

00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:07.380
According to the citizenship
laws of both India and Nepal,

00:18:07.380 --> 00:18:09.450
many Tibetans should qualify

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:11.100
for citizenship in each country,

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:12.000
either through birth

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:14.880
or through an established
connection to the country.

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:16.890
At several times in exile history,

00:18:16.890 --> 00:18:18.813
vigorous discussions have taken place

00:18:18.813 --> 00:18:22.500
as to whether or not Tibetans
should seek citizenship.

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:25.140
Each time the government has refused,

00:18:25.140 --> 00:18:28.590
but tensions surround what's
perceived to be a differential

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:30.930
application of this refusal.

00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:32.880
As one man in India said to me,

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:36.060
the Sikyong, who is the president
of the Tibetan government,

00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:37.950
and all the government ministers

00:18:37.950 --> 00:18:41.017
have citizenship and the
benefits that come with it.

00:18:41.017 --> 00:18:42.510
"Our country is Tibet,

00:18:42.510 --> 00:18:44.250
we are all fighting for it,

00:18:44.250 --> 00:18:47.130
but it is the ordinary
Tibetans in India and Nepal

00:18:47.130 --> 00:18:50.580
who make the sacrifice of
not having citizenship,"

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:51.413
end quote.

00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:54.420
In India, a handful of Tibetans

00:18:54.420 --> 00:18:56.790
have begun to make claims to citizenship

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:59.820
as provided in the
Citizenship Act of India.

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:02.430
These claims are
controversial among Tibetans

00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:05.430
as well as among Indian
government officials.

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:08.200
The high courts have approved
citizenship for Tibetans

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:11.010
only to have government offices

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:13.110
not recognize the court decisions

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:16.497
in order to prolong applicant's
imperial foreigner status.

00:19:16.497 --> 00:19:18.120
So, again, that foreigner status

00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:21.020
is something that dates back
to the time of British India.

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:23.910
Such claims are not possible in Nepal

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:25.860
where Tibetans have no name status

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:27.930
and where Chinese political influence

00:19:27.930 --> 00:19:31.200
has increased greatly since the 1990s.

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:33.030
The most recent change in India

00:19:33.030 --> 00:19:37.230
is the Tibet Rehabilitation
Policy of 2014.

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:39.330
In this policy, the government of India

00:19:39.330 --> 00:19:42.000
claimed the right to
terminate Tibetan's leases

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:44.460
and other government grants stating,

00:19:44.460 --> 00:19:46.027
here's direct quote,

00:19:46.027 --> 00:19:49.050
"The Tibetans in India
are considered refugees

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:51.480
and they are staying temporarily in India

00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:54.033
on humanitarian grounds," end quote.

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:59.310
The conservative exile government
publicly claims neutrality

00:19:59.310 --> 00:20:01.680
on citizenship for Tibetans in India,

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:05.520
stating it is a personal
decision for people to make.

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:07.980
Privately, however, everyone knows

00:20:07.980 --> 00:20:10.980
you are not supposed to
apply for citizenship.

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:15.480
Privately, people know that
to do so could cause problems.

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:18.690
What happens therefore when
Tibetans leave South Asia?

00:20:18.690 --> 00:20:21.150
What does refusal look like in countries

00:20:21.150 --> 00:20:25.530
where undocumented status is
neither allowed nor advised?

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:27.780
What options do communities have

00:20:27.780 --> 00:20:31.293
when convention refugee
status is not a possibility?

00:20:34.470 --> 00:20:39.240
Section 134 of the US
Immigration Act of 1990

00:20:39.240 --> 00:20:42.750
authorized the one time
immigration of 1,000,

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:46.140
in quotes, "displaced
Tibetans to the USA."

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:47.700
They were chosen by lottery,

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:49.920
and their families allowed
to join them in the US

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:52.230
after five years had passed.

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:54.300
This first group of Tibetan immigrants

00:20:54.300 --> 00:20:56.370
paved the way for additional groups

00:20:56.370 --> 00:20:59.460
whose imagination was
triggered by the first.

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:02.010
Following this official migration program,

00:21:02.010 --> 00:21:03.720
in the late 1990s,

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:06.180
large number numbers
of individual Tibetans

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:09.840
began to migrate to North
America on tourist visas,

00:21:09.840 --> 00:21:13.170
most flew into and
stayed in New York City,

00:21:13.170 --> 00:21:15.690
especially in Queens.

00:21:15.690 --> 00:21:17.970
And here you see a piece of art

00:21:17.970 --> 00:21:21.060
by the artist, Sodhon, from 2014.

00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:23.370
Unlike the first official group, however,

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:26.160
the later second and
third unofficial groups

00:21:26.160 --> 00:21:27.600
had nothing waiting for them

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:30.180
except for Tibetan friends
and family members.

00:21:30.180 --> 00:21:32.190
Without citizenship and working papers,

00:21:32.190 --> 00:21:34.560
they found only certain
jobs were available,

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:37.800
and over time realized
they needed citizenship.

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:41.880
However, how do you acquire
citizenship in a new country?

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:44.460
Each country's rules
on this are different.

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:47.790
In the United States, one way
you might gain citizenship

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:49.830
is via political asylum.

00:21:49.830 --> 00:21:53.220
The US government declares
five groups of people

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:54.960
eligible for asylum.

00:21:54.960 --> 00:21:57.060
Those who have suffered persecution

00:21:57.060 --> 00:22:01.140
or feel they will suffer
persecution due to race,

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:03.450
religion, nationality,

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:06.030
membership in a particular social group

00:22:06.030 --> 00:22:07.563
or political opinion.

00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:12.120
Not all Tibetans meet
those qualifications,

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:16.290
so not all qualify for
political asylum in the USA.

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:18.390
Instead, they formed a new type

00:22:18.390 --> 00:22:20.790
of citizenship migration to Canada.

00:22:20.790 --> 00:22:24.150
They have made a path from
New York City to Toronto

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:26.887
via Buffalo and Niagara Falls.

00:22:26.887 --> 00:22:29.377
"Go to Buffalo," everyone says.

00:22:29.377 --> 00:22:31.347
"You have to go to Buffalo."

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:36.630
In 2005, Kesang phoned me
in Colorado one morning

00:22:36.630 --> 00:22:37.927
from New England.

00:22:37.927 --> 00:22:39.367
"I need help," she said.

00:22:39.367 --> 00:22:41.250
"I need to go to Buffalo."

00:22:41.250 --> 00:22:42.990
She knew I had a good friend in Toronto

00:22:42.990 --> 00:22:45.427
who had made his way there via Buffalo.

00:22:45.427 --> 00:22:48.570
"Can you call him and get the
information?" she asked me.

00:22:48.570 --> 00:22:51.780
I did, and he gave me
detailed instructions.

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:54.157
And I'm gonna read them
now to you verbatim.

00:22:54.157 --> 00:22:56.917
"Take the Greyhound bus from
New York City," he said.

00:22:56.917 --> 00:22:58.740
"At the bus station in Buffalo,

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:01.350
get a taxi to Vive La Casa.

00:23:01.350 --> 00:23:03.990
The taxi drivers all know this place.

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:06.210
It should be $10 for the fair.

00:23:06.210 --> 00:23:07.530
This is a missionary shelter

00:23:07.530 --> 00:23:09.930
where she will be taken
care of by volunteers

00:23:09.930 --> 00:23:13.207
who will help her fill
out the asylum forms."

00:23:13.207 --> 00:23:15.367
"Does she speak English?" he asked me.

00:23:15.367 --> 00:23:16.717
"No," I said.

00:23:16.717 --> 00:23:18.420
"Then tell her to go with a friend.

00:23:18.420 --> 00:23:20.880
Bring her Green Book,
her (indistinct) book

00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:22.620
and Nepali passport."

00:23:22.620 --> 00:23:23.887
The story is that she,

00:23:23.887 --> 00:23:25.290
"I thought the passport

00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:27.547
that she's not really a Nepali citizen."

00:23:27.547 --> 00:23:31.407
"It's true," he said, "but
it's still hard to explain."

00:23:33.330 --> 00:23:36.330
Tibetans go to Buffalo to go to Canada.

00:23:36.330 --> 00:23:37.590
But why Canada?

00:23:37.590 --> 00:23:39.270
Why are Tibetans going to Canada

00:23:39.270 --> 00:23:41.250
rather than staying in the US?

00:23:41.250 --> 00:23:42.690
The answer is simple.

00:23:42.690 --> 00:23:46.170
In Canada, assessments for
asylum are reckoned differently,

00:23:46.170 --> 00:23:48.060
with tribunals determining or not

00:23:48.060 --> 00:23:51.870
whether Tibetans qualify for
convention refugee status,

00:23:51.870 --> 00:23:53.940
not for political asylum.

00:23:53.940 --> 00:23:57.450
Overall, Tibetans have
been deemed eligible.

00:23:57.450 --> 00:23:59.820
One thus appears before
a judge or tribunal

00:23:59.820 --> 00:24:01.200
with the need to convince them

00:24:01.200 --> 00:24:04.890
that you are indeed a bonafide Tibetan.

00:24:04.890 --> 00:24:06.840
All true or bonafide Tibetans

00:24:06.840 --> 00:24:09.408
immediately qualify for
citizenship in Canada

00:24:09.408 --> 00:24:11.700
as well as for employment assistance,

00:24:11.700 --> 00:24:15.210
English language courses,
free healthcare, and more.

00:24:15.210 --> 00:24:18.090
And, of course, any bonafide Tibetan

00:24:18.090 --> 00:24:21.753
would know the exact date of
the Dalai Lama's birthday.

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:29.040
In the 1980s, a group of Catholic nuns

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:30.720
formed a rest house for refugees

00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:33.390
fleeing war in Central
America, from Guatemala,

00:24:33.390 --> 00:24:35.730
from El Salvador, from Honduras.

00:24:35.730 --> 00:24:38.490
At the rest house, they
provided shelter, meals,

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:41.940
and clothing for the refugees,
but that was not all.

00:24:41.940 --> 00:24:44.340
They also provided free legal assistance

00:24:44.340 --> 00:24:47.220
in preparing one's asylum
application for Canada,

00:24:47.220 --> 00:24:49.140
submitted all the necessary papers

00:24:49.140 --> 00:24:51.450
and information to the
Canadian government,

00:24:51.450 --> 00:24:55.320
and then drove refugees
directly to the US-Canada border

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:58.140
once all of their documents were in place.

00:24:58.140 --> 00:25:01.323
The nuns named this
rest house Vive La Casa.

00:25:02.430 --> 00:25:04.440
On the day I arrived at Vive La Casa,

00:25:04.440 --> 00:25:06.720
my first meeting was with the director.

00:25:06.720 --> 00:25:08.910
He and I spoke for a
while about my research,

00:25:08.910 --> 00:25:10.500
then he said, "Let's go see

00:25:10.500 --> 00:25:12.540
how many Tibetans are here today."

00:25:12.540 --> 00:25:16.020
He explained there were
118 beds at Vive La Casa,

00:25:16.020 --> 00:25:18.277
and all were usually full.

00:25:18.277 --> 00:25:19.447
"Normally" he said,

00:25:19.447 --> 00:25:21.540
"anywhere from one to 10 of those beds

00:25:21.540 --> 00:25:23.587
are occupied by Tibetans."

00:25:23.587 --> 00:25:25.350
"I've worked here for four years.

00:25:25.350 --> 00:25:27.300
We've had dozens of Tibertans.

00:25:27.300 --> 00:25:30.180
They are the nicest people," end quote.

00:25:30.180 --> 00:25:31.920
We went to see the assistant director

00:25:31.920 --> 00:25:33.000
to whom he introduced me,

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:34.327
and then he asked her,

00:25:34.327 --> 00:25:36.660
"Who are our Tibetans this week?"

00:25:36.660 --> 00:25:37.890
She paused a second,

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:39.487
and then in surprise said,

00:25:39.487 --> 00:25:41.790
"There are no Tibetans here right now.

00:25:41.790 --> 00:25:44.070
One man just left for Canada this morning.

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:45.390
He was alone,

00:25:45.390 --> 00:25:49.350
and now there are no Tibetans
in the house," end quote.

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:51.270
Together, they marveled at this fact

00:25:51.270 --> 00:25:54.000
that there were no Tibetans
at Vive La Casa that morning.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:56.347
And they reassured me that, in quotes,

00:25:56.347 --> 00:25:59.340
"In recent memory,
there hasn't been a time

00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:01.407
when there were no Tibetans at Vive."

00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:07.020
So you can see here is one of
the rooms where people stay

00:26:07.020 --> 00:26:10.620
while they are waiting for
their papers to be processed.

00:26:10.620 --> 00:26:12.150
Here, you have a room for children

00:26:12.150 --> 00:26:14.730
and you can see kids as well as volunteer.

00:26:14.730 --> 00:26:19.050
Lots of very bright and
fun things to do here.

00:26:19.050 --> 00:26:21.840
And here's something that was
mentioned to me numerous times

00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:23.340
by people I spoke with in Toronto.

00:26:23.340 --> 00:26:26.550
The murals at Vive and
the importance of flags.

00:26:26.550 --> 00:26:29.310
And you can see down at the
very bottom of the E there,

00:26:29.310 --> 00:26:33.363
in the corner of the
E, is the Tibetan flag.

00:26:34.590 --> 00:26:36.960
All the staff members
had stories of Tibetans

00:26:36.960 --> 00:26:38.040
who had stayed there.

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:40.710
And almost every Tibetan in Toronto

00:26:40.710 --> 00:26:43.020
had a story of staying at Vive.

00:26:43.020 --> 00:26:44.663
They remembered particular staff members

00:26:44.663 --> 00:26:47.400
or cooking food or painting murals

00:26:47.400 --> 00:26:49.410
or picking out clothes
in the donation room

00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:51.480
before departing for Canada.

00:26:51.480 --> 00:26:53.465
They remembered also
the other refugees there

00:26:53.465 --> 00:26:55.560
from around the world.

00:26:55.560 --> 00:26:57.630
Vive La Casa director, Peter Merle,

00:26:57.630 --> 00:26:59.610
showed me the registration books

00:26:59.610 --> 00:27:01.893
for a four-day period that month.

00:27:02.820 --> 00:27:04.699
In this period, mostly individuals,

00:27:04.699 --> 00:27:08.970
but sometimes whole families
had arrived from Bangladesh,

00:27:08.970 --> 00:27:13.970
Colombia, Congo, El Salvador,
Guatemala, Haiti, Honduras,

00:27:14.010 --> 00:27:19.010
Nicaragua, Pakistan, Peru, Sri
Lanka, Trinidad and Tobago,

00:27:19.080 --> 00:27:21.847
Zimbabwe and Tibet.

00:27:21.847 --> 00:27:24.937
"There's a constant stream of
people coming in," said Peter.

00:27:24.937 --> 00:27:29.167
"And usually five to 12 people
going to Canada each day."

00:27:29.167 --> 00:27:32.190
"I say goodbye to them on
the morning they depart,

00:27:32.190 --> 00:27:35.610
and I say, 'I hope I
never see you again.'"

00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:39.813
In other words, may your asylum
application be successful.

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:43.500
But once at the border,

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:46.860
how does one prove that
you are actually Tibetan,

00:27:46.860 --> 00:27:49.530
and what does it mean to be Tibetan?

00:27:49.530 --> 00:27:51.090
Per the Canadian government,

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:52.950
it means one is a Tibetan refugee

00:27:52.950 --> 00:27:54.690
associated with the Dalai Lama

00:27:54.690 --> 00:27:57.540
and the central Tibetan
administration in exile.

00:27:57.540 --> 00:28:01.470
It mean one possesses a
Green Book, speaks Tibetan,

00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:03.840
and can narrate or otherwise perform

00:28:03.840 --> 00:28:06.330
a convincing Tibetan identity.

00:28:06.330 --> 00:28:07.950
Tibetans have their own ideas

00:28:07.950 --> 00:28:10.350
about what will best
display their Tibetansness

00:28:10.350 --> 00:28:13.650
and, of course, their ideas
do not always converge

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:17.587
with those held by Canadian
attorneys and tribunals.

00:28:17.587 --> 00:28:20.700
"Speak Tibetan and wear
a chuba for God's sake,"

00:28:20.700 --> 00:28:23.670
one attorney told me he
advised Tibetan clients.

00:28:23.670 --> 00:28:25.770
And a chuba, as you can see here,

00:28:25.770 --> 00:28:27.840
is a form of Tibetan dress.

00:28:27.840 --> 00:28:30.330
You see here two elderly women in Toronto.

00:28:30.330 --> 00:28:32.850
This is in Parkdale, the
Tibetan neighborhood.

00:28:32.850 --> 00:28:35.070
The striped aprons they
have over their chuba

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:36.930
signified that they're married women,

00:28:36.930 --> 00:28:41.370
but form of national dress
immediately recognizable.

00:28:41.370 --> 00:28:43.110
However, not just for the elderly.

00:28:43.110 --> 00:28:44.190
And here you can see,

00:28:44.190 --> 00:28:48.602
this is at the weekly Wednesday
gorshey in Toronto.

00:28:48.602 --> 00:28:51.508
Every single person
dancing in this photograph

00:28:51.508 --> 00:28:53.520
is wearing a chuba.

00:28:53.520 --> 00:28:55.800
So you can see anyone who
has that striped apron over

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:56.910
signifying married woman.

00:28:56.910 --> 00:28:59.400
The rest would be unmarried women.

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:01.920
However, this is also a
gender neutral form of dress.

00:29:01.920 --> 00:29:04.410
So here, more specifically,
you can see some men

00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:05.790
also wearing the chuba.

00:29:05.790 --> 00:29:08.130
And a couple people, some have
it up over their shoulders,

00:29:08.130 --> 00:29:09.573
some have it folded it down.

00:29:11.040 --> 00:29:13.713
Again, national dress throughout Tibet.

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:17.580
Many Tibetans who speak
English well use a translator

00:29:17.580 --> 00:29:19.230
when they go before the judge.

00:29:19.230 --> 00:29:21.270
Some have told me of the experience

00:29:21.270 --> 00:29:23.310
of the Tibetan English translator

00:29:23.310 --> 00:29:26.610
not being as fluent in
English as they are.

00:29:26.610 --> 00:29:29.190
They listen to their well
crafted answers in Tibetan

00:29:29.190 --> 00:29:31.320
be translated into a broken English.

00:29:31.320 --> 00:29:33.870
Nuances lost, details omitted,

00:29:33.870 --> 00:29:37.620
their deep answers rendered
shallow by the translator.

00:29:37.620 --> 00:29:39.000
Tibetans who do not speak English,

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:42.870
tell different stories both
of anxiety and anticipation.

00:29:42.870 --> 00:29:46.770
A shared concern all have
is with what sort of stories

00:29:46.770 --> 00:29:49.320
one is supposed to tell the judge.

00:29:49.320 --> 00:29:52.057
As a young woman
explained to me in Queens,

00:29:52.057 --> 00:29:55.107
"We don't want to lie,
but feel compelled to."

00:29:55.957 --> 00:29:59.580
"This is not," she insisted
"because Tibetans are dishonest,

00:29:59.580 --> 00:30:00.870
but because they're told

00:30:00.870 --> 00:30:03.000
they need to embellish their stories."

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:04.327
Her husband added,

00:30:04.327 --> 00:30:06.450
"Maybe they don't really need to do that,

00:30:06.450 --> 00:30:08.127
but they hear that they do."

00:30:09.390 --> 00:30:13.140
The belief that asylum seekers
lie circulates globally,

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:16.560
making refugees banal figures of distrust.

00:30:16.560 --> 00:30:17.640
In New York City,

00:30:17.640 --> 00:30:20.130
Tibetans have been placed
on the list of refugees

00:30:20.130 --> 00:30:23.040
who have known stories.

00:30:23.040 --> 00:30:26.190
in The New Yorker, author, Suketu Mehta,

00:30:26.190 --> 00:30:29.460
quotes an immigration
attorney who explains to him,

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:33.300
direct quote, "The immigration
people know all the stories.

00:30:33.300 --> 00:30:35.280
There's one for each country.

00:30:35.280 --> 00:30:37.410
There's the Colombia rape story.

00:30:37.410 --> 00:30:39.900
They all say they were raped by FARC.

00:30:39.900 --> 00:30:41.880
There's the Rwanda rape story,

00:30:41.880 --> 00:30:43.830
the Tibetan refugee story.

00:30:43.830 --> 00:30:47.610
The details for each are
the same," end quote.

00:30:47.610 --> 00:30:49.830
In Toronto, one attorney implied to me

00:30:49.830 --> 00:30:52.680
that Tibetan stories
are often seen as tame

00:30:52.680 --> 00:30:55.620
in comparison to those of others.

00:30:55.620 --> 00:30:59.610
Direct quote, "Some tribunal
members hate Tibetan cases

00:30:59.610 --> 00:31:02.430
because they come in after Rwandan guy

00:31:02.430 --> 00:31:04.533
with one arm," end quote.

00:31:05.370 --> 00:31:07.980
How can Tibetan suffering compete

00:31:07.980 --> 00:31:10.410
and how does one operate in a system

00:31:10.410 --> 00:31:13.260
that allows suffering to be competitive,

00:31:13.260 --> 00:31:17.190
or that disqualifies individuals
because of consonants

00:31:17.190 --> 00:31:18.633
in their experiences?

00:31:20.190 --> 00:31:23.190
Many Tibetans do tell the
truth in asylum court.

00:31:23.190 --> 00:31:25.140
Numerous individuals shared with me

00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:27.210
that they told their actual story

00:31:27.210 --> 00:31:29.370
against other advice they received,

00:31:29.370 --> 00:31:32.010
or that they spoke English
rather than Tibetan.

00:31:32.010 --> 00:31:34.087
As one attorney in Toronto told me,

00:31:34.087 --> 00:31:37.500
"Tibetans don't need to
lie here," end quote.

00:31:37.500 --> 00:31:40.500
Kesang's story didn't surprise me at all.

00:31:40.500 --> 00:31:43.140
Her strategy was to answer
the judge's questions

00:31:43.140 --> 00:31:44.763
as honestly as possible.

00:31:45.870 --> 00:31:49.170
She says, "I told him I
threw my fake Nepali passport

00:31:49.170 --> 00:31:50.130
into the river.

00:31:50.130 --> 00:31:52.710
I wanted to get rid of
it as fast as possible.

00:31:52.710 --> 00:31:54.960
Then he asked me about Tibetan culture,

00:31:54.960 --> 00:31:57.780
and I told him I was in a dance
troupe when I was younger.

00:31:57.780 --> 00:32:00.630
I did one of the dances for
him right in the courtroom.

00:32:00.630 --> 00:32:03.540
Oh, I told him everything, and I danced.

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:06.840
We were laughing and he
approved my refugee status."

00:32:06.840 --> 00:32:08.170
So the photo you now see

00:32:08.170 --> 00:32:11.220
is of a dance troupe that
Kesang was in back in Kathmandu.

00:32:11.220 --> 00:32:14.200
She is third one from the
left wearing the green chuba

00:32:15.161 --> 00:32:16.673
with a purple shirt underneath.

00:32:19.980 --> 00:32:22.057
So in the end, there is no punchline

00:32:22.057 --> 00:32:24.360
to the joke about the Sherpa man

00:32:24.360 --> 00:32:26.430
and the Dalai Lama's birthday.

00:32:26.430 --> 00:32:28.740
Although that story was from Toronto,

00:32:28.740 --> 00:32:31.290
similar stories circulate in New York City

00:32:31.290 --> 00:32:33.840
and throughout the Tibetan diaspora.

00:32:33.840 --> 00:32:37.440
The story about the US
embassy official in New Delhi

00:32:37.440 --> 00:32:40.260
who asked the non Tibetan posing as a monk

00:32:40.260 --> 00:32:43.200
to take off his monk's
robes and retie them.

00:32:43.200 --> 00:32:46.080
And, of course, as everyone
listening to this story knew,

00:32:46.080 --> 00:32:47.230
the man couldn't do it.

00:32:48.240 --> 00:32:50.070
The story about the asylum officer

00:32:50.070 --> 00:32:54.000
who never asks about actual
political persecution

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:56.820
but asks instead if the petitioner knows

00:32:56.820 --> 00:32:59.257
what the four noble truths are,

00:32:59.257 --> 00:33:02.070
"As if this was Toronto
rather than New York City,"

00:33:02.070 --> 00:33:04.290
says a Tibetan man in Queens.

00:33:04.290 --> 00:33:08.250
And the tale about the asylum
officer who always says no.

00:33:08.250 --> 00:33:10.050
Who says, "There is no Tibet.

00:33:10.050 --> 00:33:12.210
Tibet is a part of China,"

00:33:12.210 --> 00:33:15.690
and who never grants asylum
no matter how gruesome,

00:33:15.690 --> 00:33:19.173
how devastating, or how true one story is.

00:33:20.700 --> 00:33:23.640
Our research needs to
recognize the conditions

00:33:23.640 --> 00:33:27.120
and meet the needs of the
current political moment.

00:33:27.120 --> 00:33:29.220
This is so for our research topics,

00:33:29.220 --> 00:33:31.500
our methods, our theories.

00:33:31.500 --> 00:33:34.555
Quantitative research and
also qualitative research

00:33:34.555 --> 00:33:38.610
needs to be paired with
ethnography and vice versa.

00:33:38.610 --> 00:33:40.860
Together, we can see both big picture

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:44.760
and finer grain detail, large
structures and histories,

00:33:44.760 --> 00:33:47.280
as well as the lived everyday experience,

00:33:47.280 --> 00:33:51.540
kind of both wide angled
and zoomed in views.

00:33:51.540 --> 00:33:55.140
Ethnography is not just
another qualitative method.

00:33:55.140 --> 00:33:56.430
It is more than that.

00:33:56.430 --> 00:33:58.230
It is also a epistemology.

00:33:58.230 --> 00:34:00.600
And in that, it is theory.

00:34:00.600 --> 00:34:04.593
One theory grown from
ethnographic seeds is refusal.

00:34:05.640 --> 00:34:09.630
Refusal is insistence on the
possible over the probable.

00:34:09.630 --> 00:34:12.360
And here I think of
Isabelle Stinger's insight

00:34:12.360 --> 00:34:15.840
that if we follow
probability, there is no hope,

00:34:15.840 --> 00:34:18.600
just a calculated authorization...

00:34:18.600 --> 00:34:21.090
Sorry, a calculated anticipation

00:34:21.090 --> 00:34:23.516
authorized by the world as it is.

00:34:23.516 --> 00:34:26.970
Hope combines with will to refuse

00:34:26.970 --> 00:34:29.010
such authorized anticipations,

00:34:29.010 --> 00:34:32.700
thus moving away from the
probable into the possible.

00:34:32.700 --> 00:34:34.440
This does not happen only in courtrooms

00:34:34.440 --> 00:34:37.920
or immigration lines, but
also in everyday life.

00:34:37.920 --> 00:34:39.810
It is about joking in friendship,

00:34:39.810 --> 00:34:42.090
about recalibrating new experiences

00:34:42.090 --> 00:34:44.490
to find affordances and attunements

00:34:44.490 --> 00:34:48.570
to longstanding ways of being,
of knowing, of interacting.

00:34:48.570 --> 00:34:50.483
It is as likely to take place on sidewalks

00:34:50.483 --> 00:34:53.790
and in apartments as
an attorney's offices.

00:34:53.790 --> 00:34:56.670
Refusal is a form of hope.

00:34:56.670 --> 00:34:58.680
And Canada, over the last 15 years,

00:34:58.680 --> 00:35:00.660
I have again and again experienced this

00:35:00.660 --> 00:35:04.950
as the remaking of Canadian
places as Tibetan places,

00:35:04.950 --> 00:35:06.390
and vice versa.

00:35:06.390 --> 00:35:07.920
And so in conclusion,

00:35:07.920 --> 00:35:12.270
I want to take you to an
iconic Canadian landmark.

00:35:12.270 --> 00:35:14.433
So here we are in Tim Hortons.

00:35:15.330 --> 00:35:18.510
One summer day, Kesang
and I went to Tim Hortons,

00:35:18.510 --> 00:35:22.770
the ubiquitous Canadian donut
and coffee shop in Jameson.

00:35:22.770 --> 00:35:24.967
She said she wanted coffee.

00:35:24.967 --> 00:35:27.030
"There's better coffee
down the street," I said,

00:35:27.030 --> 00:35:28.800
thinking of CapitalEspresso

00:35:28.800 --> 00:35:31.110
down near Parkdale
Community Legal Services.

00:35:31.110 --> 00:35:32.250
A building I knew well,

00:35:32.250 --> 00:35:34.800
where some of the attorneys
with whom I work on asylum

00:35:34.800 --> 00:35:37.470
and family reunification bases were based

00:35:37.470 --> 00:35:39.000
and where signs and pamphlets

00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:40.740
were printed in numerous languages

00:35:40.740 --> 00:35:43.320
and put up on the wall
and available on counters,

00:35:43.320 --> 00:35:46.140
including in the Tibetan language.

00:35:46.140 --> 00:35:49.207
A lifelong tea drinker,
Kesang, said back to me,

00:35:49.207 --> 00:35:51.060
"I don't really care about the coffee,

00:35:51.060 --> 00:35:53.610
I want to see who is there."

00:35:53.610 --> 00:35:58.260
Continuing, she said, "Tim
Hortons is like Kora at Boudha,

00:35:58.260 --> 00:35:59.967
I see everyone I know there."

00:36:02.430 --> 00:36:04.710
For Tibetans who live
near the Boudha chorten,

00:36:04.710 --> 00:36:06.600
pictured here in Kathmandu,

00:36:06.600 --> 00:36:08.610
doing Kora or circumambulation,

00:36:08.610 --> 00:36:10.650
a form of walking meditation,

00:36:10.650 --> 00:36:13.050
around the chorten in
the morning or afternoon,

00:36:13.050 --> 00:36:16.140
or sometimes both, is a social activity

00:36:16.140 --> 00:36:18.240
as much as a religious activity.

00:36:18.240 --> 00:36:20.880
It is a main part of the day for people.

00:36:20.880 --> 00:36:23.107
Kesang laughed as she said this, saying,

00:36:23.107 --> 00:36:26.010
"There is nowhere to do Kora in Jameson."

00:36:26.010 --> 00:36:28.710
Instead she said,
"People go to Tim Hortons

00:36:28.710 --> 00:36:30.360
to see who is already there,

00:36:30.360 --> 00:36:32.940
and to hope that someone
you know will come out

00:36:32.940 --> 00:36:34.167
as you are going in."

00:36:35.220 --> 00:36:37.500
The following spring,
I was in Jameson again

00:36:37.500 --> 00:36:39.840
and stopped by the Tim Hortons on my own.

00:36:39.840 --> 00:36:42.390
Two older men were coming out,

00:36:42.390 --> 00:36:44.850
two young Tibetan women were going in.

00:36:44.850 --> 00:36:48.481
They saw a friend approaching
and they called out to her,

00:36:48.481 --> 00:36:51.183
ja btungs kha shog,
come and drink tea.

00:36:52.620 --> 00:36:55.380
So come and drink tea.

00:36:55.380 --> 00:36:58.500
This is the practice
of refugee citizenship.

00:36:58.500 --> 00:37:02.250
This is refusal as
possibility still unfolding

00:37:02.250 --> 00:37:03.690
in this world.

00:37:03.690 --> 00:37:04.523
Thank you.

00:37:06.750 --> 00:37:10.110
So maybe we can start with
questions from the audience.

00:37:10.110 --> 00:37:11.340
- [Attendee] Who's inspired you

00:37:11.340 --> 00:37:14.746
to start working with
the Tibetan community,

00:37:14.746 --> 00:37:18.303
(indistinct) in South Asia or in general?

00:37:19.364 --> 00:37:20.940
- I get asked that question a lot.

00:37:20.940 --> 00:37:21.773
Thank you for that.

00:37:21.773 --> 00:37:23.220
So when I was in college,

00:37:23.220 --> 00:37:24.810
I wanted to study abroad.

00:37:24.810 --> 00:37:28.530
And I had never left the
United States before.

00:37:28.530 --> 00:37:31.863
And I learned of a study
abroad program to Nepal.

00:37:32.790 --> 00:37:34.920
And I remember the...

00:37:34.920 --> 00:37:37.380
The first line said, "Nepal
is a country of superlatives."

00:37:37.380 --> 00:37:38.850
And it went on to talk about the country

00:37:38.850 --> 00:37:39.750
as this, you know,

00:37:39.750 --> 00:37:42.090
from sea level to the
top of Mount Everest,

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:44.490
Hinduism, Buddhism, 40
different languages spoken

00:37:44.490 --> 00:37:47.160
in a country the size
of Tennessee, basically.

00:37:47.160 --> 00:37:51.090
And I was captivated and
decided to go there, and did.

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:54.935
And in the very first weeks
of the program, they took us,

00:37:54.935 --> 00:37:58.560
so we were a group of maybe
20 US college students,

00:37:58.560 --> 00:38:01.113
up to a village close to
the border with Tibet.

00:38:01.113 --> 00:38:03.090
So for anyone who's familiar with Nepal,

00:38:03.090 --> 00:38:05.460
we were in Marpha, which
is just south of Jomsom,

00:38:05.460 --> 00:38:07.515
which is just south of Mustang.

00:38:07.515 --> 00:38:09.870
And we lived there with families

00:38:09.870 --> 00:38:11.220
and studied the Nepali language.

00:38:11.220 --> 00:38:13.410
So we stayed there for two weeks,

00:38:13.410 --> 00:38:15.990
right below, kind of 10 minute walk

00:38:15.990 --> 00:38:19.140
from the village of Marpha
was a Tibetan refugee camp.

00:38:19.140 --> 00:38:20.370
And this was on a trekking route,

00:38:20.370 --> 00:38:22.260
and the way you trucking Nepal

00:38:22.260 --> 00:38:24.360
is where you go hiking
from village to village

00:38:24.360 --> 00:38:26.370
and stay with families
each night and guest house.

00:38:26.370 --> 00:38:27.203
Well, back in the day.

00:38:27.203 --> 00:38:29.093
Now, you might just
stay in a little hotel.

00:38:29.093 --> 00:38:33.690
And you never spent really
more than one night in a place,

00:38:33.690 --> 00:38:35.550
and traveling usually in small groups.

00:38:35.550 --> 00:38:37.980
So because we were staying and
we were a group of students

00:38:37.980 --> 00:38:40.644
learning to speak Nepali, we
became a tourist attraction.

00:38:40.644 --> 00:38:41.880
So people would come

00:38:41.880 --> 00:38:44.730
to see this strange group
of American students

00:38:44.730 --> 00:38:48.720
who were living in the village
and learning to speak Nepali.

00:38:48.720 --> 00:38:52.047
And what the Tibetan
men and women told me,

00:38:52.047 --> 00:38:54.630
and they would come each day
to hang out in the afternoon,

00:38:54.630 --> 00:38:56.257
drink tea, and just chat,

00:38:56.257 --> 00:38:58.800
changed the whole course of my life.

00:38:58.800 --> 00:39:01.770
So again, I was 20 years old at the time,

00:39:01.770 --> 00:39:04.020
and they told me they
had lost their country,

00:39:04.020 --> 00:39:05.280
and that they couldn't go back.

00:39:05.280 --> 00:39:07.980
At the time, there was no
way to make a phone call

00:39:07.980 --> 00:39:10.890
or to send a letter to Tibet,

00:39:10.890 --> 00:39:13.140
so the men and women I was talking to

00:39:13.140 --> 00:39:16.560
said we left behind our parents
and our brothers and sisters

00:39:16.560 --> 00:39:17.940
and friends and grandparents,

00:39:17.940 --> 00:39:21.300
and this had been 30
years since they had left.

00:39:21.300 --> 00:39:24.210
They had no communication
at all with their family.

00:39:24.210 --> 00:39:27.180
And that alone blew my mind, right?

00:39:27.180 --> 00:39:28.170
At the time, there was one place

00:39:28.170 --> 00:39:29.670
you could make a phone call in Kathmandu,

00:39:29.670 --> 00:39:30.900
but I could go there and pay money

00:39:30.900 --> 00:39:33.210
and call my parents if I wanted to.

00:39:33.210 --> 00:39:35.280
So that was important, but
it had never occurred to me

00:39:35.280 --> 00:39:37.080
you could lose your country.

00:39:37.080 --> 00:39:38.220
And for anyone in this room

00:39:38.220 --> 00:39:40.890
who works in the field of refugee studies,

00:39:40.890 --> 00:39:42.994
that is often the position that
most refugees are in, right?

00:39:42.994 --> 00:39:45.960
It also hadn't occurred
to them necessarily

00:39:45.960 --> 00:39:47.580
like at that initial moment

00:39:47.580 --> 00:39:49.290
that they were going to become refugees.

00:39:49.290 --> 00:39:51.780
Or even that after the
initial becoming a refugee,

00:39:51.780 --> 00:39:54.254
that they weren't going to
be going back to a renewed

00:39:54.254 --> 00:39:57.930
and safe landscape in
just a matter of months.

00:39:57.930 --> 00:40:00.150
And so I wanted to know,
well, what happened and why

00:40:00.150 --> 00:40:02.490
and why don't I know about this?

00:40:02.490 --> 00:40:05.100
And so that was the start of me

00:40:05.100 --> 00:40:07.230
trying to learn what had happened,

00:40:07.230 --> 00:40:10.440
to educate myself, and to
share that story with others.

00:40:10.440 --> 00:40:13.437
And here I am 30 years later.

00:40:13.437 --> 00:40:15.175
Tibetans now can make phone calls

00:40:15.175 --> 00:40:17.760
and can maybe send some packages.

00:40:17.760 --> 00:40:19.860
Everything is heavily
surveilled though and censored,

00:40:19.860 --> 00:40:21.420
so you have to be very careful.

00:40:21.420 --> 00:40:24.390
But there is at least
communication now between families.

00:40:24.390 --> 00:40:28.263
But for the first three
decades, there was none at all.

00:40:30.840 --> 00:40:32.700
And imagine living with that, right?

00:40:32.700 --> 00:40:35.475
What would it be like to
live your life like that

00:40:35.475 --> 00:40:37.650
not knowing the fate of your family,

00:40:37.650 --> 00:40:39.753
not being able to just check in?

00:40:41.070 --> 00:40:43.710
I mean, think of how connected
we are with cell phones now

00:40:43.710 --> 00:40:45.150
and how many times...

00:40:45.150 --> 00:40:46.590
Think of the people you you connect

00:40:46.590 --> 00:40:48.420
with every single day through your phone

00:40:48.420 --> 00:40:52.860
or some sort of digital form of technology

00:40:52.860 --> 00:40:54.799
even if you don't see them face to face.

00:40:54.799 --> 00:40:56.880
What would it be like to live without that

00:40:56.880 --> 00:40:58.230
and without knowing?

00:40:58.230 --> 00:41:00.720
So that's how I got started.

00:41:00.720 --> 00:41:02.270
It made a big impression on me.

00:41:05.370 --> 00:41:06.920
- [Attendee] Thank you so much.

00:41:07.816 --> 00:41:10.028
I have a little bit more specific question

00:41:10.028 --> 00:41:11.100
in the case of India

00:41:11.100 --> 00:41:13.350
and what you were saying
about like resident,

00:41:15.176 --> 00:41:18.510
or residence, and how that sort of process

00:41:18.510 --> 00:41:22.670
of applying for that works,
and like who gets that?

00:41:22.670 --> 00:41:23.610
And also have you encountered people

00:41:23.610 --> 00:41:26.163
who have obtained Indian citizenship?

00:41:27.510 --> 00:41:28.710
- So, yes.

00:41:28.710 --> 00:41:30.390
And as that one man said,

00:41:30.390 --> 00:41:32.880
that Tibetan government officials had it

00:41:32.880 --> 00:41:35.145
because they had to be able
to travel internationally.

00:41:35.145 --> 00:41:37.440
And some of them I think also appreciated

00:41:37.440 --> 00:41:38.820
the perks that came with it, right?

00:41:38.820 --> 00:41:40.080
Like, the rights that you do have.

00:41:40.080 --> 00:41:41.760
If you have a passport,

00:41:41.760 --> 00:41:44.430
or in this country,
write a driver's license,

00:41:44.430 --> 00:41:46.980
those markers, those documents
of citizenship rights

00:41:46.980 --> 00:41:47.813
come with it.

00:41:47.813 --> 00:41:50.430
If you are stateless
with no documentation,

00:41:50.430 --> 00:41:51.960
you literally have zero rights, right?

00:41:51.960 --> 00:41:54.180
Convention refugee status
gives you some rights,

00:41:54.180 --> 00:41:56.853
again, but not available
to Tibetans in India.

00:41:57.780 --> 00:42:01.860
So an RC or an IC
registration certificates.

00:42:01.860 --> 00:42:03.570
And I say, I mean, I can
give you the details later.

00:42:03.570 --> 00:42:05.460
There are specific thresholds like,

00:42:05.460 --> 00:42:08.460
if you or your parents came
here before a certain date,

00:42:08.460 --> 00:42:11.340
so there's certain things like
that that you have to go by.

00:42:11.340 --> 00:42:15.333
So many Tibetans, but not
all do have these in India.

00:42:16.530 --> 00:42:18.330
Foreigner's Registration Certificates

00:42:18.330 --> 00:42:20.520
require renewing annually.

00:42:20.520 --> 00:42:21.360
So you have to go.

00:42:21.360 --> 00:42:26.360
It often requires also paying
bribes to get that work done.

00:42:26.370 --> 00:42:29.010
Or traveling to wherever
the nearest office is

00:42:29.010 --> 00:42:31.383
that is going to renew this for you.

00:42:32.670 --> 00:42:34.080
So in Nepal, it's not that way.

00:42:34.080 --> 00:42:36.360
In India, there are
forms of documentation.

00:42:36.360 --> 00:42:39.540
These are not necessarily
recognized outside of India.

00:42:39.540 --> 00:42:43.950
So the Indian government will
issue a travel kind of visa,

00:42:43.950 --> 00:42:47.280
or a travel stamp on your
identity certificate.

00:42:47.280 --> 00:42:49.320
And actually in the longer
version of this talk,

00:42:49.320 --> 00:42:50.460
I think maybe I cut too much.

00:42:50.460 --> 00:42:52.170
Seems like we have a lot of time left.

00:42:52.170 --> 00:42:54.360
I talk about a Tibetan woman in Toronto

00:42:54.360 --> 00:42:55.530
who traveled to Europe

00:42:55.530 --> 00:43:00.000
on this Indian issued,
government issued identities, IC,

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:02.640
identity certificate that
had a stamp on it saying

00:43:02.640 --> 00:43:03.940
she could travel internationally.

00:43:03.940 --> 00:43:05.640
And she said she landed in Europe,

00:43:05.640 --> 00:43:08.550
and the immigration officials
like made fun of her.

00:43:08.550 --> 00:43:10.380
We're like calling people
over to like, "Look at this."

00:43:10.380 --> 00:43:12.930
And she just felt this sense of shame.

00:43:12.930 --> 00:43:16.134
And so some of the
conversations I've had with her

00:43:16.134 --> 00:43:20.130
are about how she wants to
impart secure citizenship

00:43:20.130 --> 00:43:23.370
and a sense of security
and safety for her children

00:43:23.370 --> 00:43:26.370
so that they don't go through
what it is she's gone through.

00:43:28.410 --> 00:43:29.243
But the difference

00:43:29.243 --> 00:43:33.077
kind of just to fully pull
in the idea of refusal

00:43:33.077 --> 00:43:36.138
than between, say, her experience in India

00:43:36.138 --> 00:43:39.240
and her experience now in Canada, right?

00:43:39.240 --> 00:43:41.988
Where she has received
citizenship, not refused it, right?

00:43:41.988 --> 00:43:45.810
So refusing citizenship
for Tibetans in India

00:43:45.810 --> 00:43:48.360
has allowed them for a very long time now,

00:43:48.360 --> 00:43:51.300
or it's been 63 years,

00:43:51.300 --> 00:43:54.150
to argue that they are
not accepting citizenship

00:43:54.150 --> 00:43:58.710
in India or Nepal because
they are citizens of Tibet.

00:43:58.710 --> 00:44:01.410
It's just Tibet is colonized
right now by China, right?

00:44:01.410 --> 00:44:05.370
So they can't activate to
realize that citizenship

00:44:05.370 --> 00:44:07.853
in that way, but it's kind
of this deferral, right?

00:44:08.760 --> 00:44:09.593
So you might say,

00:44:09.593 --> 00:44:10.520
"Okay, well then if you take citizenship

00:44:10.520 --> 00:44:12.954
in the USA or Canada or
France or Switzerland

00:44:12.954 --> 00:44:14.908
or all these other countries

00:44:14.908 --> 00:44:18.625
that don't allow you to live
in an undocumented status

00:44:18.625 --> 00:44:21.960
for multiple generations,

00:44:21.960 --> 00:44:24.423
how does that impact your claim to Tibet?

00:44:25.290 --> 00:44:28.110
And so the argument that is made

00:44:28.110 --> 00:44:31.320
is that it enables us to
fight for that claim, right?

00:44:31.320 --> 00:44:33.890
It enables us to fight for
that Tibetan citizenship.

00:44:33.890 --> 00:44:34.860
So in essence,

00:44:34.860 --> 00:44:37.863
it's a form of dual
citizenship as I understand it.

00:44:40.230 --> 00:44:42.600
The acquiring of Canadian citizenship

00:44:42.600 --> 00:44:47.580
or of American citizenship
is not the loss or denial

00:44:47.580 --> 00:44:49.860
or forfeiture of Tibetan citizenship,

00:44:49.860 --> 00:44:51.660
but it's now a dual sort of thing.

00:44:51.660 --> 00:44:53.040
Like, now with the backing

00:44:53.040 --> 00:44:56.973
of not just a US or Canadian passport,

00:44:58.560 --> 00:45:00.840
but also that security, right?

00:45:00.840 --> 00:45:03.060
And the lack of shame, the lack of stigma,

00:45:03.060 --> 00:45:05.640
the ability to act in a new way.

00:45:05.640 --> 00:45:07.260
And so that's why that line about,

00:45:07.260 --> 00:45:10.260
it's kind of the goal is the same,

00:45:10.260 --> 00:45:11.910
like, the argument shifts, right?

00:45:11.910 --> 00:45:14.261
So when refusal has that malleability

00:45:14.261 --> 00:45:17.010
in terms of what are the
political needs of the moment

00:45:17.010 --> 00:45:19.953
and why is it perhaps this is arising now.

00:45:24.750 --> 00:45:26.160
- [Attendee] I an avid reader of yours.

00:45:26.160 --> 00:45:30.240
It's such a privilege and
delight to hear your person.

00:45:30.240 --> 00:45:32.970
So thanks so much for making this happen.

00:45:32.970 --> 00:45:34.370
I actually wanted to...

00:45:35.759 --> 00:45:37.980
I have two sort of questions about refusal

00:45:37.980 --> 00:45:40.590
as both a concept and as a practice.

00:45:40.590 --> 00:45:43.410
And I really appreciate building on

00:45:43.410 --> 00:45:45.990
and sort of also taking
diverse directions,

00:45:45.990 --> 00:45:48.273
existing families, fellowship on refusal.

00:45:49.260 --> 00:45:50.093
So I wanted to...

00:45:50.093 --> 00:45:52.380
If you could speak to one,

00:45:52.380 --> 00:45:56.613
which is, as ethnographer yourself,

00:45:59.257 --> 00:46:04.257
what is your entry point into refusal

00:46:05.336 --> 00:46:07.410
in your own position as a ethnographer

00:46:07.410 --> 00:46:09.690
who's sustained these relationships,

00:46:09.690 --> 00:46:13.920
maintain them, committed to
them for so many years now?

00:46:13.920 --> 00:46:18.683
So are you approaching that
mentality from the perspective

00:46:21.550 --> 00:46:24.090
(speaker drowns out by another speaker)

00:46:24.090 --> 00:46:26.340
who are doing this kind of refusal work?

00:46:26.340 --> 00:46:28.650
Are you also, as an ethnographer,

00:46:28.650 --> 00:46:32.130
participating in it, against
it, within, despite it?

00:46:32.130 --> 00:46:34.630
So that's one question I
would like you to answer.

00:46:39.180 --> 00:46:43.497
And secondly, within these
communities of exile or refugee

00:46:43.497 --> 00:46:48.497
(indistinct) in places outside Tibet.

00:46:52.420 --> 00:46:56.280
Other sort of conflicts
about that refusal,

00:46:56.280 --> 00:46:59.542
whether it is gendered or
about positions of class,

00:46:59.542 --> 00:47:02.430
like, within the communities itself,

00:47:02.430 --> 00:47:06.600
what are the kinds of
contestations about refusal

00:47:06.600 --> 00:47:09.213
if they're acknowledging
it as sort of one practice?

00:47:10.230 --> 00:47:11.463
Yeah, those two things.

00:47:13.050 --> 00:47:14.520
- So starting with the first.

00:47:14.520 --> 00:47:15.960
Let's go in order.

00:47:15.960 --> 00:47:20.820
So back to refusal as a
stance, as well as a theory,

00:47:20.820 --> 00:47:24.200
and not everyone might be
fully familiar with the work,

00:47:24.200 --> 00:47:27.030
so this is really drawing
on the work of Audra Simpson

00:47:27.030 --> 00:47:30.163
who I mentioned, for
whom ethnographic refusal

00:47:30.163 --> 00:47:32.580
is not just the study of refusal

00:47:32.580 --> 00:47:34.680
as a political practice by a community,

00:47:34.680 --> 00:47:37.225
but also an option of the scholar

00:47:37.225 --> 00:47:40.275
to not disclose certain
ethnographic details

00:47:40.275 --> 00:47:43.367
or stories or even arguments, right?

00:47:43.367 --> 00:47:46.140
That those must be held.

00:47:46.140 --> 00:47:49.740
And she posits this as not
just the sort of decisions

00:47:49.740 --> 00:47:51.210
we all have to make as authors.

00:47:51.210 --> 00:47:52.710
In writing, you can't put everything in,

00:47:52.710 --> 00:47:54.570
so you choose to leave some stuff out.

00:47:54.570 --> 00:47:55.420
This is not that.

00:47:56.430 --> 00:48:00.040
This is, again, a willful
conscious decision

00:48:01.320 --> 00:48:05.010
to leave out things that a
reader would expect, right?

00:48:05.010 --> 00:48:07.380
That scholarship would demand,

00:48:07.380 --> 00:48:10.241
that people expect to know
based on the research you did,

00:48:10.241 --> 00:48:12.750
that you say, "I'm not going to tell you."

00:48:12.750 --> 00:48:16.290
I'm not going to give you the
information that ethnography

00:48:16.290 --> 00:48:17.370
or whatever your field is,

00:48:17.370 --> 00:48:20.730
political science, history, sociology,

00:48:20.730 --> 00:48:25.320
has historically demanded
to know based on research.

00:48:25.320 --> 00:48:26.910
So that just to share with folks

00:48:26.910 --> 00:48:29.373
who aren't familiar with Audra's work.

00:48:30.780 --> 00:48:34.350
For me, historically, I
have worked with refusal

00:48:34.350 --> 00:48:35.820
as a practice of the community

00:48:35.820 --> 00:48:37.770
because that's where I
first encountered it.

00:48:37.770 --> 00:48:40.020
And originally I wasn't
doing research on this.

00:48:40.020 --> 00:48:42.240
It was just kept coming
up again and again.

00:48:42.240 --> 00:48:45.003
Even before I started
the research on asylum,

00:48:46.110 --> 00:48:49.420
I was so struck by the way
that people I interacted with

00:48:50.520 --> 00:48:53.223
consistently used the word, we refused it.

00:48:54.075 --> 00:48:55.560
That was the term they use

00:48:55.560 --> 00:48:57.060
when they were talking about citizenship.

00:48:57.060 --> 00:48:58.500
And speaking in the collective,

00:48:58.500 --> 00:48:59.910
not speaking individually.

00:48:59.910 --> 00:49:01.950
So I came to it that way,

00:49:01.950 --> 00:49:04.383
and that was initially
the way that I used it.

00:49:05.250 --> 00:49:06.480
Familiar with Audra's work,

00:49:06.480 --> 00:49:09.240
familiar with the idea of
refusal as ethnographic stands,

00:49:09.240 --> 00:49:11.640
but, for me, that has
only come very recently,

00:49:11.640 --> 00:49:13.320
and I'll just quickly share the story

00:49:13.320 --> 00:49:16.590
of how I've used ethnographic
refusal in my research

00:49:16.590 --> 00:49:18.780
'cause I do think it's relevant.

00:49:18.780 --> 00:49:20.850
And that is in...

00:49:20.850 --> 00:49:22.080
Just in this last year.

00:49:22.080 --> 00:49:24.930
So one year ago, actually
it was November, 2021,

00:49:24.930 --> 00:49:28.290
I gave a lecture at the AAA's in Baltimore

00:49:28.290 --> 00:49:30.690
in which I told the
story of a woman, right?

00:49:30.690 --> 00:49:34.380
It was kind of the ethnographic
biography of one person.

00:49:34.380 --> 00:49:37.350
And someone who had been
a difficult personality,

00:49:37.350 --> 00:49:40.203
who had lived a difficult
and a very sad life.

00:49:41.280 --> 00:49:43.440
And I told her story.

00:49:43.440 --> 00:49:44.910
Maybe a couple things left out,

00:49:44.910 --> 00:49:46.770
not necessarily for ethnographic refusal,

00:49:46.770 --> 00:49:48.873
more for respective for care.

00:49:50.100 --> 00:49:51.660
And I had told her story,

00:49:51.660 --> 00:49:53.426
she had recently passed away,

00:49:53.426 --> 00:49:55.200
in conjunction with her family.

00:49:55.200 --> 00:49:57.270
So her family knew I was telling her story

00:49:57.270 --> 00:49:58.470
and had worked with me on...

00:49:58.470 --> 00:50:01.320
They hadn't read it 'cause
they didn't want to,

00:50:01.320 --> 00:50:03.150
but they knew I was doing this.

00:50:03.150 --> 00:50:06.430
Afterwards, as I was preparing
this lecture for publication

00:50:07.410 --> 00:50:10.185
and was finalizing it,

00:50:10.185 --> 00:50:11.700
and you might know just how
long this all takes, right?

00:50:11.700 --> 00:50:15.300
Like, to come up with a long research talk

00:50:15.300 --> 00:50:17.850
to convert it to an article.

00:50:17.850 --> 00:50:19.563
I stumbled across a photo of this woman

00:50:19.563 --> 00:50:20.970
that I had forgotten about

00:50:20.970 --> 00:50:23.347
and I sent it to someone
in her family, like,

00:50:23.347 --> 00:50:25.997
"Oh, look, I just found this
old photo of so and so."

00:50:27.081 --> 00:50:28.410
And that person...

00:50:28.410 --> 00:50:33.410
Something in that photo
triggered something in her

00:50:34.590 --> 00:50:36.210
and she wrote back to me,

00:50:36.210 --> 00:50:37.207
and then we talked and she said,

00:50:37.207 --> 00:50:40.233
"I don't want the story to
be out in the world anymore,"

00:50:41.610 --> 00:50:44.527
and, "Can you please not write it?"

00:50:45.747 --> 00:50:47.523
And I wrote back immediately said,

00:50:48.440 --> 00:50:49.273
"I will not tell the..."

00:50:49.273 --> 00:50:50.106
I mean, I can tell the story

00:50:50.106 --> 00:50:51.600
of not telling the story, right?

00:50:51.600 --> 00:50:54.123
But I said, "If you don't
want me to tell the story,

00:50:54.123 --> 00:50:56.610
I won't, and I'm not going to,

00:50:56.610 --> 00:50:59.220
and I probably will make
that promise to you."

00:50:59.220 --> 00:51:01.239
And I had already presented it publicly,

00:51:01.239 --> 00:51:04.590
it was already up online as a video.

00:51:04.590 --> 00:51:05.730
Lots of people had watched it,

00:51:05.730 --> 00:51:07.890
lots of people had been in the audience.

00:51:07.890 --> 00:51:09.390
And I contacted...

00:51:09.390 --> 00:51:10.970
It was AES.

00:51:10.970 --> 00:51:12.180
So AES had had put it up.

00:51:12.180 --> 00:51:13.920
It was the distinguished lecture for AES.

00:51:13.920 --> 00:51:14.753
The video was up and I said,

00:51:14.753 --> 00:51:16.560
"I need you to take down the video."

00:51:16.560 --> 00:51:19.080
And the article was due in like two days.

00:51:19.080 --> 00:51:20.523
And I wrote to the editors
of the journal and I said,

00:51:20.523 --> 00:51:21.813
"I'm really sorry.

00:51:23.693 --> 00:51:26.310
I was going to be ready to send it to you,

00:51:26.310 --> 00:51:27.690
but I can't send this to you now

00:51:27.690 --> 00:51:29.310
and I need to take some
time to rethink this."

00:51:29.310 --> 00:51:30.880
I was scheduled to give a
talk on it in California.

00:51:30.880 --> 00:51:32.743
I had to contact those people, and I said,

00:51:32.743 --> 00:51:35.910
"I can't come present this talk,

00:51:35.910 --> 00:51:36.900
and I need to regroup

00:51:36.900 --> 00:51:38.610
and see if I have anything
to present to you."

00:51:38.610 --> 00:51:40.830
I took a week to just kind of ponder,

00:51:40.830 --> 00:51:42.900
ponder and think about what to do.

00:51:42.900 --> 00:51:46.470
And I ended up coming up
with a plan how I could...

00:51:46.470 --> 00:51:48.480
What happens is that her story

00:51:48.480 --> 00:51:51.780
was the heart of the talk I had given.

00:51:51.780 --> 00:51:53.310
And I had to go deep into my notes,

00:51:53.310 --> 00:51:56.684
like, into my field
notes going back decades,

00:51:56.684 --> 00:52:01.343
as well as into my relationships myself.

00:52:01.343 --> 00:52:03.870
And I did find stories to tell.

00:52:03.870 --> 00:52:05.730
And so the article now exists.

00:52:05.730 --> 00:52:06.780
If you can go and read it,

00:52:06.780 --> 00:52:10.567
it came out in August in AE.

00:52:10.567 --> 00:52:14.280
And the article is
called Theory as Ethics,

00:52:14.280 --> 00:52:16.830
is this particular article.

00:52:16.830 --> 00:52:19.590
It was about this,
theory from ethnography.

00:52:19.590 --> 00:52:22.830
It was about relationships being
at the heart of what we do,

00:52:22.830 --> 00:52:25.980
and what does it mean to not tell a story

00:52:25.980 --> 00:52:30.150
that the discipline
would expect me to tell.

00:52:30.150 --> 00:52:30.983
And actually...

00:52:30.983 --> 00:52:33.660
So when I did go to
California and I presented it

00:52:33.660 --> 00:52:35.040
to an interdisciplinary audience,

00:52:35.040 --> 00:52:37.137
and I kind of shared
like as I'm doing now,

00:52:37.137 --> 00:52:39.097
afterwards, I had someone tell me,

00:52:39.097 --> 00:52:41.937
"You could have told the
story anyways of that woman."

00:52:43.329 --> 00:52:44.941
Just not getting it,

00:52:44.941 --> 00:52:49.890
not understanding the
ethics of what it means

00:52:49.890 --> 00:52:53.470
to refuse to tell something
that's been trusted to you

00:52:56.010 --> 00:52:57.870
because it would cause pain in the world,

00:52:57.870 --> 00:53:00.143
because someone else
doesn't need to know, right?

00:53:01.415 --> 00:53:03.120
What is our right to know?

00:53:03.120 --> 00:53:06.510
So, second question, refresh my memory,

00:53:06.510 --> 00:53:07.343
and then I'll...

00:53:07.343 --> 00:53:08.520
Sorry, that was a long answer.

00:53:08.520 --> 00:53:10.671
You didn't know what you
were asking when you-

00:53:10.671 --> 00:53:11.760
(attendee faintly speaking)

00:53:11.760 --> 00:53:13.658
- [Attendee] The second
was about contestations

00:53:13.658 --> 00:53:14.586
within the community on refusal.

00:53:14.586 --> 00:53:15.960
- Oh, very few.

00:53:15.960 --> 00:53:18.961
The Tibetan community
is incredibly united.

00:53:18.961 --> 00:53:23.940
There's not so much
contestation as there is,

00:53:23.940 --> 00:53:25.203
frankly, jealousy.

00:53:26.280 --> 00:53:30.120
So folks inside India,
especially in India,

00:53:30.120 --> 00:53:32.340
hold tight to the not gaining citizenship.

00:53:32.340 --> 00:53:35.040
Nepal again was never an issue.

00:53:35.040 --> 00:53:37.740
So it's not like you could have opinion

00:53:37.740 --> 00:53:38.573
one way or the other,

00:53:38.573 --> 00:53:41.414
whereas in India, there is
an active conversation on it.

00:53:41.414 --> 00:53:43.220
It's more...

00:53:44.280 --> 00:53:45.916
Now, there's a wistfulness.

00:53:45.916 --> 00:53:49.620
Oh, look at those two buttons
in X country who have it.

00:53:49.620 --> 00:53:53.220
And then you have folks
perhaps in Sunnyside

00:53:53.220 --> 00:53:54.720
or Long Island City saying,

00:53:54.720 --> 00:53:56.103
Oh, back in India we had the following,

00:53:56.103 --> 00:53:59.640
and there's wistfulness and nostalgia

00:53:59.640 --> 00:54:01.470
over sense of community

00:54:01.470 --> 00:54:03.450
and things that were
positive about that, right?

00:54:03.450 --> 00:54:04.803
So there's ways, again,

00:54:05.640 --> 00:54:10.110
there's refusals in wanting
to cross certain lines, right?

00:54:10.110 --> 00:54:11.550
But that is very much a discourse

00:54:11.550 --> 00:54:12.690
within the Tibetan community.

00:54:12.690 --> 00:54:13.800
What does it mean to be united?

00:54:13.800 --> 00:54:16.650
What does it mean to have an
active political struggle?

00:54:16.650 --> 00:54:19.218
And the community is
incredibly united on that.

00:54:19.218 --> 00:54:22.098
You had your hand up as well.

00:54:22.098 --> 00:54:22.931
- [Attendee] Yes, thank you.

00:54:22.931 --> 00:54:23.764
Very fascinating talk.

00:54:23.764 --> 00:54:25.110
Thank you.

00:54:25.110 --> 00:54:26.130
Two quick questions also.

00:54:26.130 --> 00:54:27.300
So one is about roots.

00:54:27.300 --> 00:54:28.440
I'm interested in...

00:54:28.440 --> 00:54:31.920
I can see that Vive La
Casa is a very central note

00:54:31.920 --> 00:54:35.370
in this move from America to Canada,

00:54:35.370 --> 00:54:37.093
but are they also moving into Canada

00:54:37.093 --> 00:54:39.909
straight from South Asia,

00:54:39.909 --> 00:54:41.721
or does it have to go-

00:54:41.721 --> 00:54:43.877
- No, they all come in
through the United States.

00:54:43.877 --> 00:54:45.177
- [Attendee] Right, right.

00:54:46.590 --> 00:54:48.623
And why is that the case?

00:54:48.623 --> 00:54:51.149
- Because this is individual immigration.

00:54:51.149 --> 00:54:52.200
So unless...

00:54:52.200 --> 00:54:56.100
So recently, there was
a citizen organized,

00:54:56.100 --> 00:54:58.650
Canadian government
approved immigration plan

00:54:58.650 --> 00:55:00.060
to bring over a group of Tibetans

00:55:00.060 --> 00:55:02.010
from Arunachal Pradesh.

00:55:02.010 --> 00:55:04.920
So that group came in directly from India.

00:55:04.920 --> 00:55:07.380
And same as the group of
1000 I was talking about

00:55:07.380 --> 00:55:08.940
before that took place in
the United States, right?

00:55:08.940 --> 00:55:10.860
They came in, they had a plan,

00:55:10.860 --> 00:55:13.410
they had people waiting for
them, Canadian citizens,

00:55:13.410 --> 00:55:15.930
many of whom were Tibetan
Canadian citizens, jobs.

00:55:15.930 --> 00:55:17.373
So that sort of thing.

00:55:18.570 --> 00:55:21.780
That's a group organized immigration

00:55:21.780 --> 00:55:23.370
and authorized by the government.

00:55:23.370 --> 00:55:24.330
This is different.

00:55:24.330 --> 00:55:25.800
These are individual people

00:55:25.800 --> 00:55:29.010
appearing at the border
petitioning for asylum.

00:55:29.010 --> 00:55:30.060
And so to do that,

00:55:30.060 --> 00:55:32.047
you cannot do that at a high commission.

00:55:32.047 --> 00:55:34.200
It's same for the United
States as for Canada,

00:55:34.200 --> 00:55:36.000
you can't go to a US
embassy around the world

00:55:36.000 --> 00:55:36.870
and request asylum.

00:55:36.870 --> 00:55:39.120
You can only request
that either at the border

00:55:39.120 --> 00:55:41.423
when you're entering, or
once you're in country,

00:55:41.423 --> 00:55:43.863
there's a period of time which you can.

00:55:45.570 --> 00:55:46.920
- [Attendee] And then related to that,

00:55:46.920 --> 00:55:48.780
the argument about refusal,

00:55:48.780 --> 00:55:52.200
is it that they are refusing
citizenship in South Asia

00:55:52.200 --> 00:55:54.477
or are you arguing that
they're refusing the status

00:55:54.477 --> 00:55:59.088
of being undocumented and
regulated in South Asia,

00:55:59.088 --> 00:56:01.278
or is it a possibility
of getting citizenship?

00:56:01.278 --> 00:56:03.030
So I'm not entirely clear,

00:56:03.030 --> 00:56:06.143
but it's 'cause I don't know
much about the context's,

00:56:06.143 --> 00:56:07.410
whether you are arguing

00:56:07.410 --> 00:56:10.770
they're refusing citizenship
in South Asia, in India,

00:56:10.770 --> 00:56:14.883
or the status of not being
documented and regulated.

00:56:15.720 --> 00:56:17.850
- They're refusing citizenship.

00:56:17.850 --> 00:56:21.068
So since 1959, so now we're on third,

00:56:21.068 --> 00:56:24.330
even fourth generation of
Tibetans have lived in India

00:56:24.330 --> 00:56:28.170
with either, again, it's not
just undocumented or under,

00:56:28.170 --> 00:56:29.520
because some of them do have

00:56:29.520 --> 00:56:32.460
these government of India
issued documentations

00:56:32.460 --> 00:56:33.860
that are good only in India.

00:56:35.640 --> 00:56:38.160
The question for me actually is,

00:56:38.160 --> 00:56:40.020
what were they offered?

00:56:40.020 --> 00:56:42.840
And the details of this
have never been made public

00:56:42.840 --> 00:56:44.430
either for his holiness,

00:56:44.430 --> 00:56:48.210
the Dalai Lama's relationships
with Nairu in the 1950s

00:56:48.210 --> 00:56:50.850
or with the King of Nepal in the 1950s,

00:56:50.850 --> 00:56:52.803
where Tibetans offered citizenship.

00:56:54.000 --> 00:56:56.054
That, I don't know for
sure because, again,

00:56:56.054 --> 00:56:58.733
I don't have access to the
agreements that were made.

00:56:58.733 --> 00:57:02.700
The way that Tibetans
publicly and privately speak

00:57:02.700 --> 00:57:04.413
is that, we refused it.

00:57:05.640 --> 00:57:08.343
So it's not a protest
of being undocumented,

00:57:09.420 --> 00:57:11.460
it's an acceptance of that.

00:57:11.460 --> 00:57:15.390
But positing it as we
are refusing citizenship

00:57:15.390 --> 00:57:17.510
regardless of whether or
not it's offered to us.

00:57:17.510 --> 00:57:19.452
Again, per the Constitution of India,

00:57:19.452 --> 00:57:22.710
many Tibetans qualify for citizenship.

00:57:22.710 --> 00:57:24.270
However, the government of India

00:57:24.270 --> 00:57:27.090
also doesn't recognize this.

00:57:27.090 --> 00:57:28.680
Believe me, I have to
explain this all the time

00:57:28.680 --> 00:57:30.630
to judges in Canada and the United States.

00:57:30.630 --> 00:57:32.790
So it's like, "Well, look
right here in the constitution,

00:57:32.790 --> 00:57:33.780
a Tibetan would qualify."

00:57:33.780 --> 00:57:37.920
I'm like, I understand it says
that and it looks that way,

00:57:37.920 --> 00:57:39.409
but that is not the practice,

00:57:39.409 --> 00:57:44.010
which again is why ethnography
and lived experience

00:57:44.010 --> 00:57:45.900
is as important, if not more important,

00:57:45.900 --> 00:57:48.195
sometimes than what of a losses,

00:57:48.195 --> 00:57:52.277
because actual experience
does not correspond here

00:57:52.277 --> 00:57:55.143
with what the legal
documents say should happen.

00:57:56.220 --> 00:57:58.080
- [Eda] There are two questions.

00:57:58.080 --> 00:57:59.010
- Okay.

00:57:59.010 --> 00:58:01.050
- [Eda] So one is that,

00:58:01.050 --> 00:58:05.820
as we got to know about the
1951 Refugee Convention,

00:58:05.820 --> 00:58:07.920
the question that they're asking is,

00:58:07.920 --> 00:58:11.220
why the refugee laws came
just after World War II,

00:58:11.220 --> 00:58:13.353
but not just after the 1940s?

00:58:14.520 --> 00:58:18.690
And can we look at that
act as something driven?

00:58:18.690 --> 00:58:22.713
It's a general question about
the 1951 Refugee Convention.

00:58:26.520 --> 00:58:29.194
- I'm not sure if the questions
for the world in general

00:58:29.194 --> 00:58:30.660
or for South Asia.

00:58:30.660 --> 00:58:33.000
I mean, the answer for both is yes.

00:58:33.000 --> 00:58:36.835
I mean, the formulation of
refugee laws and of UNHCR

00:58:36.835 --> 00:58:39.578
following World War II
was an attempt to solve

00:58:39.578 --> 00:58:41.820
what was seen as the crisis of refugees

00:58:41.820 --> 00:58:43.395
and stateless peoples in the world

00:58:43.395 --> 00:58:44.670
following World War II.

00:58:44.670 --> 00:58:47.010
I feel like this is
stuff many people know.

00:58:47.010 --> 00:58:50.670
And the agreement among countries

00:58:50.670 --> 00:58:54.180
that every piece of territory
had to be associated

00:58:54.180 --> 00:58:55.919
to a nation state, right?

00:58:55.919 --> 00:58:58.200
So there couldn't be a patch of land

00:58:58.200 --> 00:59:01.980
that somehow was
independent of some state,

00:59:01.980 --> 00:59:06.980
and that is where international
refugee law comes from.

00:59:07.770 --> 00:59:10.500
In South Asia, the
decision not to sign on,

00:59:10.500 --> 00:59:14.730
again, as a collectivity,
as a group of states,

00:59:14.730 --> 00:59:15.932
clearly was a political decision

00:59:15.932 --> 00:59:19.080
that they have held to ever since.

00:59:19.080 --> 00:59:22.920
So to whoever is asking,
the answer is yes.

00:59:22.920 --> 00:59:25.410
What that means for
refugees in South Asia?

00:59:25.410 --> 00:59:27.210
If you recall the statement that I read

00:59:27.210 --> 00:59:28.340
from the Tibetan Policy Act

00:59:28.340 --> 00:59:31.803
is the government of India
considers Tibetans refugees.

00:59:32.758 --> 00:59:37.410
So per government of India
terminology, they are refugees,

00:59:37.410 --> 00:59:39.330
but that does not align them

00:59:39.330 --> 00:59:41.670
with UN convention refugee status,

00:59:41.670 --> 00:59:44.703
which is then a shared series
of rights or set of rights

00:59:44.703 --> 00:59:47.010
that are supposed to be upheld

00:59:47.010 --> 00:59:49.958
by countries around the
world that are actionable.

00:59:49.958 --> 00:59:52.400
This is a different type of...

00:59:53.790 --> 00:59:55.433
- [Eda] Yeah, Estrella, go ahead.

00:59:57.533 --> 00:59:59.340
- [Estrella] In the book,

00:59:59.340 --> 01:00:02.670
you talk about the
journeys of Latin refugees

01:00:02.670 --> 01:00:05.798
through Nepal and Arunachal
and their encounter

01:00:05.798 --> 01:00:09.180
with different religions,
different cuisine,

01:00:09.180 --> 01:00:12.630
and sort of how that
experience to move for them

01:00:12.630 --> 01:00:16.890
was like a world expanding
experience culturally.

01:00:16.890 --> 01:00:18.960
And like I was struck by that

01:00:18.960 --> 01:00:20.992
when you were talking about (indistinct)

01:00:20.992 --> 01:00:22.740
and stuff like that.

01:00:22.740 --> 01:00:25.410
And I was wondering specifically like,

01:00:25.410 --> 01:00:27.450
what have you observed

01:00:27.450 --> 01:00:29.640
or what have the people you've spoken with

01:00:29.640 --> 01:00:33.114
observed about themselves
in terms of social rules,

01:00:33.114 --> 01:00:37.000
attitudes, behaviors, that
remained sticky for them

01:00:37.000 --> 01:00:40.322
as it was when they lived at home,

01:00:40.322 --> 01:00:42.489
versus after all of these years of life

01:00:42.489 --> 01:00:44.084
living in a different place

01:00:44.084 --> 01:00:45.985
and navigating different cultures.

01:00:45.985 --> 01:00:48.837
and this can be in
(indistinct) like gender

01:00:48.837 --> 01:00:51.963
or even like teaching or
socializing or whatever.

01:00:53.790 --> 01:00:54.623
- I guess two things.

01:00:54.623 --> 01:00:57.093
One, Tibetans are incredibly cosmopolitan.

01:00:58.500 --> 01:01:00.714
So Tibetans live in multiple
countries around the world,

01:01:00.714 --> 01:01:02.872
as well as the bulk of Tibetans

01:01:02.872 --> 01:01:05.010
still to this day live inside Tibet

01:01:05.010 --> 01:01:07.110
as part of the People's Republic of China.

01:01:07.950 --> 01:01:10.830
And each year, more and more Tibetans

01:01:10.830 --> 01:01:14.743
come from Tibet outside,
especially for education.

01:01:14.743 --> 01:01:17.310
And so there's lots of movement.

01:01:17.310 --> 01:01:19.170
This is a community that,

01:01:19.170 --> 01:01:22.756
while for a long time was
very much only in Asia

01:01:22.756 --> 01:01:24.270
for the last 20 years,

01:01:24.270 --> 01:01:27.480
has become really truly
an international diaspora.

01:01:27.480 --> 01:01:29.620
So that sense of cosmopolitanism,

01:01:29.620 --> 01:01:32.555
whereas if we think of what
was it that enabled China,

01:01:32.555 --> 01:01:34.470
what was one of the things anyways,

01:01:34.470 --> 01:01:36.420
that enabled China to take over Tibet,

01:01:36.420 --> 01:01:38.160
to just march right in and invade was,

01:01:38.160 --> 01:01:40.410
Tibet didn't have diplomatic
relations with any countries

01:01:40.410 --> 01:01:41.403
except for Nepal.

01:01:42.240 --> 01:01:45.270
So, I mean, it was the opposite
of a cosmopolitan country.

01:01:45.270 --> 01:01:47.636
It was very much
insulated, inward focused,

01:01:47.636 --> 01:01:50.970
looking at Buddhism,
it had not yet entered

01:01:50.970 --> 01:01:52.800
the world of modern diplomacy

01:01:52.800 --> 01:01:55.500
in terms of building relationships
with other countries,

01:01:55.500 --> 01:01:57.030
and so there were no countries

01:01:57.030 --> 01:02:01.290
who then had those sort
of diplomatic obligations

01:02:01.290 --> 01:02:02.130
to step in.

01:02:02.130 --> 01:02:03.063
So there's that.

01:02:04.590 --> 01:02:06.872
But in thinking specifically
about South Asia,

01:02:06.872 --> 01:02:09.930
which is where Tibetans came into, 1959,

01:02:09.930 --> 01:02:13.946
and really up until the year 2000,

01:02:13.946 --> 01:02:17.569
the overwhelming majority
of Tibetan in exile

01:02:17.569 --> 01:02:19.293
were in South Asia.

01:02:20.790 --> 01:02:23.980
One of the big things I think
that that has been overlooked

01:02:24.930 --> 01:02:29.930
has been a conservativism in
gender expression and roles.

01:02:30.330 --> 01:02:33.600
So actually a shrinking of
possibilities for women,

01:02:33.600 --> 01:02:35.910
and I think that is
something that has emerged

01:02:35.910 --> 01:02:38.400
from coming into society dominated

01:02:38.400 --> 01:02:42.582
by kind of Hindu and Muslim wars,

01:02:42.582 --> 01:02:46.205
and a much more conservative
public profile for women

01:02:46.205 --> 01:02:51.205
than what Tibetans had
experienced prior to that.

01:02:53.040 --> 01:02:54.390
That's something I've thought about.

01:02:54.390 --> 01:02:57.118
No one to my knowledge has
actively researched it,

01:02:57.118 --> 01:02:59.160
but that's one thing.

01:02:59.160 --> 01:03:00.885
What does it mean to come
into a different society

01:03:00.885 --> 01:03:03.827
where rules for even just
like what parts of the body

01:03:03.827 --> 01:03:05.820
need to be covered when
you appear in public,

01:03:05.820 --> 01:03:09.270
what's considered decors or not right

01:03:09.270 --> 01:03:12.150
for a woman to how she
comports herself in public,

01:03:12.150 --> 01:03:13.842
things like that.

01:03:13.842 --> 01:03:16.358
So South Asia has been very influential

01:03:16.358 --> 01:03:18.851
on the Tibetan community.

01:03:18.851 --> 01:03:22.500
Anyone who grew up there

01:03:22.500 --> 01:03:25.440
can probably sing and dance
any number of Bollywood songs

01:03:25.440 --> 01:03:27.120
going all the way back decades.

01:03:27.120 --> 01:03:30.690
So like, there's the
influences that are beloved,

01:03:30.690 --> 01:03:33.243
and that are charming and that are fun.

01:03:34.260 --> 01:03:36.480
Culinary influences as well.

01:03:36.480 --> 01:03:37.530
And then there's the ones

01:03:37.530 --> 01:03:39.780
that fly a little bit lower
under the surface, I think,

01:03:39.780 --> 01:03:41.820
such as this, conservativism,

01:03:41.820 --> 01:03:44.730
not that Tibet wasn't a
conservative society earlier,

01:03:44.730 --> 01:03:47.370
but I think it manifested
itself in new ways in exile,

01:03:47.370 --> 01:03:50.010
and gender, I believe, is one of them.

01:03:50.010 --> 01:03:51.120
- [Eda] One more question.

01:03:51.120 --> 01:03:54.600
- We have one question.

01:03:54.600 --> 01:03:55.433
- Sure.

01:03:58.170 --> 01:04:00.953
- [Staff] John, if you
want to unmute yourself

01:04:00.953 --> 01:04:02.433
and ask the question.

01:04:03.720 --> 01:04:04.950
- [John] Yes.

01:04:04.950 --> 01:04:07.980
In the 1960s, one of the
first groups of Tibetans

01:04:07.980 --> 01:04:10.680
to leave South Asia went to Switzerland.

01:04:10.680 --> 01:04:12.960
Do you know what happened to those people

01:04:12.960 --> 01:04:15.180
and how much of a
Tibetan presence there is

01:04:15.180 --> 01:04:16.383
in Switzerland today?

01:04:17.940 --> 01:04:18.773
- I do.

01:04:18.773 --> 01:04:19.860
They are still there.

01:04:19.860 --> 01:04:23.700
So until very recently,
Switzerland, you're right,

01:04:23.700 --> 01:04:27.559
had the longest standing kind
of oldest Tibetan community.

01:04:27.559 --> 01:04:29.940
It was kind of Switzerland and the UK,

01:04:29.940 --> 01:04:31.350
although the number of Tibetans in the UK

01:04:31.350 --> 01:04:34.740
was also always much smaller
than the number of Switzerland.

01:04:34.740 --> 01:04:36.750
So it's a thriving community now,

01:04:36.750 --> 01:04:38.160
and it's now a community.

01:04:38.160 --> 01:04:41.540
And Tibetans actually are kind
of throughout from Geneva...

01:04:43.920 --> 01:04:45.540
All sorts of cities in Switzerland,

01:04:45.540 --> 01:04:48.457
centered in the Zurich
area and (indistinct),

01:04:48.457 --> 01:04:50.700
and that part of the country.

01:04:50.700 --> 01:04:52.610
But now is a community that,

01:04:52.610 --> 01:04:54.488
kind of like the community
in India and Nepal,

01:04:54.488 --> 01:04:58.320
is three generations deep.

01:04:58.320 --> 01:05:02.250
So fully, younger
generation is fully immersed

01:05:02.250 --> 01:05:03.690
into Swiss life,

01:05:03.690 --> 01:05:06.300
whether it's Swiss French or Swiss German,

01:05:06.300 --> 01:05:09.270
and a very successful community as well.

01:05:09.270 --> 01:05:13.170
So just prior to the
pandemic, I was working on,

01:05:13.170 --> 01:05:15.900
I was going to be adding
France and Switzerland

01:05:15.900 --> 01:05:18.240
to my study of refugee
citizenship around the world,

01:05:18.240 --> 01:05:21.000
and I planned on spending
the summer of 2020

01:05:21.000 --> 01:05:22.500
half in Paris and half in Zurich,

01:05:22.500 --> 01:05:23.597
and then (indistinct).

01:05:25.740 --> 01:05:28.389
The situation changed, but,
so I'm hoping to go back.

01:05:28.389 --> 01:05:31.590
So very active community there,

01:05:31.590 --> 01:05:34.830
but a community that is now truly Swiss,

01:05:34.830 --> 01:05:36.210
as well as Tibet,

01:05:36.210 --> 01:05:38.640
and yet maintaining active ties

01:05:38.640 --> 01:05:40.650
to Tibetans around the world, right?

01:05:40.650 --> 01:05:42.330
There's no Tibetan...

01:05:42.330 --> 01:05:44.370
I can't think of any
Tibetan friend of mine

01:05:44.370 --> 01:05:47.520
or colleague I know who
doesn't have family members

01:05:47.520 --> 01:05:49.531
in multiple countries
around the world, right?

01:05:49.531 --> 01:05:52.830
So my Swiss friends,
Swiss Tibetan friends,

01:05:52.830 --> 01:05:55.800
have relatives in D.C.
and relatives in Delhi

01:05:55.800 --> 01:05:58.053
and families still inside Tibet.

01:05:59.100 --> 01:06:03.450
So an important country for Tibetans

01:06:03.450 --> 01:06:05.747
because Tibetans have been residents there

01:06:05.747 --> 01:06:07.523
since the early '60.

01:06:07.523 --> 01:06:10.590
Again, one of those first
places outside of South Asia,

01:06:10.590 --> 01:06:13.563
and still an active and vibrant community.

01:06:14.400 --> 01:06:15.500
- [Attendee] Thank you.

01:06:15.500 --> 01:06:17.517
- Yeah, you're welcome.

01:06:17.517 --> 01:06:19.260
- [Staff] You wanna close it or...

01:06:19.260 --> 01:06:20.443
All right.

01:06:20.443 --> 01:06:22.350
- That's the question.
- Oh, great.

01:06:22.350 --> 01:06:24.030
I have been thinking...

01:06:24.030 --> 01:06:29.030
I've been thinking about
both your and Audra's concept

01:06:30.028 --> 01:06:33.048
of refusal as sort of
historically contingent modes

01:06:33.048 --> 01:06:38.048
of agency that are made
differential available

01:06:38.280 --> 01:06:39.210
to different people.

01:06:39.210 --> 01:06:42.835
And I was struck by
essentially what you said,

01:06:42.835 --> 01:06:46.320
essentially in the global
south, in South Asia,

01:06:46.320 --> 01:06:49.200
the refusal is available as...

01:06:49.200 --> 01:06:51.716
Because being undocumented
and underdocumented

01:06:51.716 --> 01:06:53.820
is a possibility of life,

01:06:53.820 --> 01:06:56.550
whereas in the global
north where it is not,

01:06:56.550 --> 01:06:58.050
refusal looks different.

01:06:58.050 --> 01:07:00.630
And so then I think with
Catherine Besteman's

01:07:00.630 --> 01:07:04.410
sort of militarized global apartheid,

01:07:04.410 --> 01:07:07.230
and I'm wondering how the Tibetan

01:07:07.230 --> 01:07:11.220
sort of situation and this sort
of different modes of agency

01:07:11.220 --> 01:07:14.190
or different modes of
refusal co-articulate

01:07:14.190 --> 01:07:16.650
with this militarized global apartheid

01:07:16.650 --> 01:07:19.980
to also bring in your wonderful
book, "Arrested Histories,"

01:07:19.980 --> 01:07:23.220
which deals with the CIA
history and so forth.

01:07:23.220 --> 01:07:24.053
But, yeah.

01:07:25.140 --> 01:07:26.220
- Oh, absolutely.

01:07:26.220 --> 01:07:27.450
I mean, articulates it.

01:07:27.450 --> 01:07:30.243
And I think what comes to me initially

01:07:30.243 --> 01:07:32.374
is not from my own
research, but from Audra.

01:07:32.374 --> 01:07:35.160
So the scholar we're
referring to, Audrey Simpson,

01:07:35.160 --> 01:07:37.980
has a book called "Mohawk Interruptus."

01:07:37.980 --> 01:07:39.750
And one of the things she
talks about in this book

01:07:39.750 --> 01:07:42.690
is not just her own crossing
of the US-Canadian border,

01:07:42.690 --> 01:07:46.620
but of the Mohawk, the Iroquois
National Lacrosse Team,

01:07:46.620 --> 01:07:47.676
traveling to South America

01:07:47.676 --> 01:07:50.108
and insisting on using their
Iroquois Nation passports,

01:07:50.108 --> 01:07:51.840
and then trying to come back into.

01:07:51.840 --> 01:07:54.570
So they received, they can
go to South America on those,

01:07:54.570 --> 01:07:56.520
but when they tried to
come back in, right?

01:07:56.520 --> 01:07:58.137
And what does it mean to say...

01:07:58.137 --> 01:08:00.390
And they, of course, also

01:08:00.390 --> 01:08:02.490
have a US or have a Canadian passport,

01:08:02.490 --> 01:08:03.323
what does it mean to say,

01:08:03.323 --> 01:08:04.950
I'm not going to use that one?

01:08:04.950 --> 01:08:06.180
I think this gets into it directly.

01:08:06.180 --> 01:08:08.640
Like, who is allowed to make what choices?

01:08:08.640 --> 01:08:09.960
Who is allowed to refuse what?

01:08:09.960 --> 01:08:12.450
And this is why refusal, I think,

01:08:12.450 --> 01:08:14.280
as so many of us are articulating it

01:08:14.280 --> 01:08:16.110
is different than resistance, right?

01:08:16.110 --> 01:08:19.590
Resistance requires that
relationship of subjugation, right?

01:08:19.590 --> 01:08:22.650
It unfolds in a landscape that's unequal.

01:08:22.650 --> 01:08:24.349
Subordination is part of resistance.

01:08:24.349 --> 01:08:26.160
You are pushing back.

01:08:26.160 --> 01:08:28.588
Refusal is a horizontal relationship.

01:08:28.588 --> 01:08:32.220
Like, I have a US passport,
I'm not going to use it.

01:08:32.220 --> 01:08:37.220
I'm going to insist right on
using this other passport.

01:08:37.290 --> 01:08:38.820
And what does it take?

01:08:38.820 --> 01:08:40.500
This is back to that Tibetan woman

01:08:40.500 --> 01:08:43.080
who felt the shame and the
stigma of showing up that like,

01:08:43.080 --> 01:08:46.350
what does it take to have
that sort of sensibility

01:08:46.350 --> 01:08:48.600
to be able to refuse publicly

01:08:48.600 --> 01:08:51.270
when facing a security officer?

01:08:51.270 --> 01:08:52.200
Like, what are the stakes,

01:08:52.200 --> 01:08:53.490
what are the repercussions,

01:08:53.490 --> 01:08:57.490
when can you make that
move and when can you not?

01:08:57.490 --> 01:08:59.790
Because there certainly
are some situations

01:08:59.790 --> 01:09:01.863
in which that is not an option.

01:09:02.790 --> 01:09:05.910
And resistance might not be
either in certain states.

01:09:05.910 --> 01:09:08.895
But I would say, absolutely
they're similar things

01:09:08.895 --> 01:09:11.284
with deep resonance.

01:09:11.284 --> 01:09:12.390
- [Attendee] It's helpful

01:09:12.390 --> 01:09:16.770
because the cyclical question of agency

01:09:16.770 --> 01:09:19.353
that keeps coming every 20, 30 years,

01:09:20.460 --> 01:09:23.640
concepts like refusal help
us provide a topography

01:09:23.640 --> 01:09:26.250
of different modes that is not...

01:09:26.250 --> 01:09:28.920
Do these people have agency or don't they?

01:09:28.920 --> 01:09:30.188
So thank you for that.

01:09:30.188 --> 01:09:31.500
- Oh, sure.

01:09:31.500 --> 01:09:32.640
Well, thank you all,

01:09:32.640 --> 01:09:35.760
and thank you folks who
were watching online.

01:09:35.760 --> 01:09:37.187
Yeah, glad you were all here.

01:09:37.187 --> 01:09:40.187
(crowd applauding)

